[{:db/id 17592186157445 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ccdd18fc-f395-5a18-a92a-23c7269d12d7" "question-6cd0f9e1-610c-5585-9b7b-d28d8445a663" "question-0512b677-42e3-54b0-95e1-80a717c5187c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ccdd18fc-f395-5a18-a92a-23c7269d12d7" :question/id #uuid "ccdd18fc-f395-5a18-a92a-23c7269d12d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80912" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-21a921da-7c82-54fc-aa04-c89d5a085fc7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-21a921da-7c82-54fc-aa04-c89d5a085fc7" :answer-field/id #uuid "21a921da-7c82-54fc-aa04-c89d5a085fc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f8e27a72-2946-59da-bcf0-3181c7880bea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3ac6361a-3666-51e1-ad3a-ea67da87f711" "answer-f8e27a72-2946-59da-bcf0-3181c7880bea" "answer-978db9fb-d665-57b9-9fe1-32f7e55f2d9e" "answer-87a2e203-b50f-5dbf-9bd4-c890f1e18dcf" "answer-06dd0bfa-9489-5a0b-9a26-72dd4e5382d1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ac6361a-3666-51e1-ad3a-ea67da87f711" :answer/id #uuid "3ac6361a-3666-51e1-ad3a-ea67da87f711" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8e27a72-2946-59da-bcf0-3181c7880bea" :answer/id #uuid "f8e27a72-2946-59da-bcf0-3181c7880bea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-978db9fb-d665-57b9-9fe1-32f7e55f2d9e" :answer/id #uuid "978db9fb-d665-57b9-9fe1-32f7e55f2d9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87a2e203-b50f-5dbf-9bd4-c890f1e18dcf" :answer/id #uuid "87a2e203-b50f-5dbf-9bd4-c890f1e18dcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06dd0bfa-9489-5a0b-9a26-72dd4e5382d1" :answer/id #uuid "06dd0bfa-9489-5a0b-9a26-72dd4e5382d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6cd0f9e1-610c-5585-9b7b-d28d8445a663" :question/id #uuid "6cd0f9e1-610c-5585-9b7b-d28d8445a663" :question/math-academy-id "q-80599" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dcb4ea53-f6ce-50a3-91c6-7ce13b2fa771"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dcb4ea53-f6ce-50a3-91c6-7ce13b2fa771" :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb4ea53-f6ce-50a3-91c6-7ce13b2fa771" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-440ab6ac-9050-5cbf-8d75-c724eb062d9b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0bba6a2c-47a1-5b62-98ad-01578dba4908" "answer-1b168faa-7cfe-5aab-9d7c-288148211462" "answer-440ab6ac-9050-5cbf-8d75-c724eb062d9b" "answer-785d1da2-9cd4-54fc-b182-e308cb8c3a12" "answer-c08d5be1-ef64-5ba5-adca-0ebd93c83b4a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bba6a2c-47a1-5b62-98ad-01578dba4908" :answer/id #uuid "0bba6a2c-47a1-5b62-98ad-01578dba4908" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{t}^{2}{e}^{-4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b168faa-7cfe-5aab-9d7c-288148211462" :answer/id #uuid "1b168faa-7cfe-5aab-9d7c-288148211462" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-440ab6ac-9050-5cbf-8d75-c724eb062d9b" :answer/id #uuid "440ab6ac-9050-5cbf-8d75-c724eb062d9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-785d1da2-9cd4-54fc-b182-e308cb8c3a12" :answer/id #uuid "785d1da2-9cd4-54fc-b182-e308cb8c3a12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c08d5be1-ef64-5ba5-adca-0ebd93c83b4a" :answer/id #uuid "c08d5be1-ef64-5ba5-adca-0ebd93c83b4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0512b677-42e3-54b0-95e1-80a717c5187c" :question/id #uuid "0512b677-42e3-54b0-95e1-80a717c5187c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80908" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-08fed612-7f25-5560-bb83-15a960a92bfa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-08fed612-7f25-5560-bb83-15a960a92bfa" :answer-field/id #uuid "08fed612-7f25-5560-bb83-15a960a92bfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-99ddea77-e1d5-55f5-a432-e6303cb4cb21" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a7622813-e693-5336-bdfb-7190ca932526" "answer-bfa84c58-35f5-52bb-a6b3-d6cd3ea0ee3a" "answer-99ddea77-e1d5-55f5-a432-e6303cb4cb21" "answer-a2cb13b6-b0d6-5d1c-b058-05a7573d9e6d" "answer-827bb430-5de2-5c2c-852f-2f50416777f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7622813-e693-5336-bdfb-7190ca932526" :answer/id #uuid "a7622813-e693-5336-bdfb-7190ca932526" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/3},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfa84c58-35f5-52bb-a6b3-d6cd3ea0ee3a" :answer/id #uuid "bfa84c58-35f5-52bb-a6b3-d6cd3ea0ee3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99ddea77-e1d5-55f5-a432-e6303cb4cb21" :answer/id #uuid "99ddea77-e1d5-55f5-a432-e6303cb4cb21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2cb13b6-b0d6-5d1c-b058-05a7573d9e6d" :answer/id #uuid "a2cb13b6-b0d6-5d1c-b058-05a7573d9e6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-827bb430-5de2-5c2c-852f-2f50416777f8" :answer/id #uuid "827bb430-5de2-5c2c-852f-2f50416777f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157446 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c42bb2d4-33f1-5601-a069-ea5fef63539f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c42bb2d4-33f1-5601-a069-ea5fef63539f" :question/id #uuid "c42bb2d4-33f1-5601-a069-ea5fef63539f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326237" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4-4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8ffbd89f-d981-524e-9095-fdb0dc1768f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8ffbd89f-d981-524e-9095-fdb0dc1768f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ffbd89f-d981-524e-9095-fdb0dc1768f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ffb02d5f-3761-5753-87fa-efd0f4de2d82" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f239d215-8bad-5494-ad9f-a7e4dc4827bd" "answer-dd60fa24-2113-573f-927a-cc5cc09eff1c" "answer-afc1305e-c6de-5db9-8835-27482372478c" "answer-ffb02d5f-3761-5753-87fa-efd0f4de2d82" "answer-6123a8e7-8566-539c-a378-251b6266a004"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f239d215-8bad-5494-ad9f-a7e4dc4827bd" :answer/id #uuid "f239d215-8bad-5494-ad9f-a7e4dc4827bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd60fa24-2113-573f-927a-cc5cc09eff1c" :answer/id #uuid "dd60fa24-2113-573f-927a-cc5cc09eff1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afc1305e-c6de-5db9-8835-27482372478c" :answer/id #uuid "afc1305e-c6de-5db9-8835-27482372478c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffb02d5f-3761-5753-87fa-efd0f4de2d82" :answer/id #uuid "ffb02d5f-3761-5753-87fa-efd0f4de2d82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6123a8e7-8566-539c-a378-251b6266a004" :answer/id #uuid "6123a8e7-8566-539c-a378-251b6266a004" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157447 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d67f8554-75f7-5018-9643-79d6cf0e83b6" "question-a4894a4b-386a-5db4-8e7b-0e56f6d08b5c" "question-4a2a9a41-3861-52b6-aa0d-2940105366a8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d67f8554-75f7-5018-9643-79d6cf0e83b6" :question/id #uuid "d67f8554-75f7-5018-9643-79d6cf0e83b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80929" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-3.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4+3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-80a2d0fe-c382-5881-8e02-d1c86d71cb09"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-80a2d0fe-c382-5881-8e02-d1c86d71cb09" :answer-field/id #uuid "80a2d0fe-c382-5881-8e02-d1c86d71cb09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e930397d-0699-53ec-a3a5-3a49c82b1c8a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c30eff0d-ffd1-5d17-bc6f-a345783aae21" "answer-e582a2bf-4e2c-5f80-aa69-4386efa886fd" "answer-cf4d4ff6-2575-5aa5-adaf-1dfeb10d5b6f" "answer-e930397d-0699-53ec-a3a5-3a49c82b1c8a" "answer-db46ec5a-9287-56d8-98f8-2de1ee93eb52"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c30eff0d-ffd1-5d17-bc6f-a345783aae21" :answer/id #uuid "c30eff0d-ffd1-5d17-bc6f-a345783aae21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1+t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e582a2bf-4e2c-5f80-aa69-4386efa886fd" :answer/id #uuid "e582a2bf-4e2c-5f80-aa69-4386efa886fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf4d4ff6-2575-5aa5-adaf-1dfeb10d5b6f" :answer/id #uuid "cf4d4ff6-2575-5aa5-adaf-1dfeb10d5b6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e930397d-0699-53ec-a3a5-3a49c82b1c8a" :answer/id #uuid "e930397d-0699-53ec-a3a5-3a49c82b1c8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db46ec5a-9287-56d8-98f8-2de1ee93eb52" :answer/id #uuid "db46ec5a-9287-56d8-98f8-2de1ee93eb52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a4894a4b-386a-5db4-8e7b-0e56f6d08b5c" :question/id #uuid "a4894a4b-386a-5db4-8e7b-0e56f6d08b5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80617" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5-5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d46ab32-dd48-56fe-af69-855cd332f49c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d46ab32-dd48-56fe-af69-855cd332f49c" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d46ab32-dd48-56fe-af69-855cd332f49c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6bed8f55-cb39-5a3b-86c2-5cd8c26c256c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b6ce04dc-14c5-5800-b05b-94c7bf45b6c1" "answer-a90db176-5114-5ca8-a22e-ebcadf82891a" "answer-31af8209-d6f7-5861-bb8f-000ec6cd874e" "answer-d8570b37-63eb-524c-ba0e-1892354b0a05" "answer-6bed8f55-cb39-5a3b-86c2-5cd8c26c256c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6ce04dc-14c5-5800-b05b-94c7bf45b6c1" :answer/id #uuid "b6ce04dc-14c5-5800-b05b-94c7bf45b6c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{5t} \\\\ (t+2){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a90db176-5114-5ca8-a22e-ebcadf82891a" :answer/id #uuid "a90db176-5114-5ca8-a22e-ebcadf82891a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}(t-1) \\\\ (2-t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31af8209-d6f7-5861-bb8f-000ec6cd874e" :answer/id #uuid "31af8209-d6f7-5861-bb8f-000ec6cd874e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}(t-1){e}^{5t} \\\\ 2{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8570b37-63eb-524c-ba0e-1892354b0a05" :answer/id #uuid "d8570b37-63eb-524c-ba0e-1892354b0a05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6bed8f55-cb39-5a3b-86c2-5cd8c26c256c" :answer/id #uuid "6bed8f55-cb39-5a3b-86c2-5cd8c26c256c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4a2a9a41-3861-52b6-aa0d-2940105366a8" :question/id #uuid "4a2a9a41-3861-52b6-aa0d-2940105366a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-80616" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-1{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15c8b6c9-4d90-5de4-bd62-0285c561ccb6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15c8b6c9-4d90-5de4-bd62-0285c561ccb6" :answer-field/id #uuid "15c8b6c9-4d90-5de4-bd62-0285c561ccb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6015db17-0453-5d51-9f6c-9b63c750a7e5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4e8a7671-a4f9-5cb6-aad5-295efc3dd7c4" "answer-f5ea50a5-4a7b-5fa7-80c3-71388673c51f" "answer-7d81a220-377a-5acf-8093-4fcf21b38c96" "answer-6015db17-0453-5d51-9f6c-9b63c750a7e5" "answer-2466ac54-a28c-5e81-8865-e657fdfdddf6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e8a7671-a4f9-5cb6-aad5-295efc3dd7c4" :answer/id #uuid "4e8a7671-a4f9-5cb6-aad5-295efc3dd7c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+2t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2t-1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5ea50a5-4a7b-5fa7-80c3-71388673c51f" :answer/id #uuid "f5ea50a5-4a7b-5fa7-80c3-71388673c51f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+\\frac{t}{2}){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2t-1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d81a220-377a-5acf-8093-4fcf21b38c96" :answer/id #uuid "7d81a220-377a-5acf-8093-4fcf21b38c96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6015db17-0453-5d51-9f6c-9b63c750a7e5" :answer/id #uuid "6015db17-0453-5d51-9f6c-9b63c750a7e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2466ac54-a28c-5e81-8865-e657fdfdddf6" :answer/id #uuid "2466ac54-a28c-5e81-8865-e657fdfdddf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068910 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2100ce73-4770-5a89-a8b5-3f4b9726facd" "question-585630ea-edc2-5584-9703-3115d4a68d23"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068546 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $0$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{-}}f(x) = 1 \\, .\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{+}}f(x) = 1 \\, .\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{+}}f(x)=1 \\,.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068911 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f85c0d08-ef45-5aca-a806-01e365550a2b" "question-c6b36716-81d8-575e-98da-f48320938f53"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068649 :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ (shown below) is defined on $x\\in [-2,2]$. Find the left-sided and right-sided limits for the function at $x=-2$ and $x=2$. \n\n![](images/96/963e4b2e7875c5f8156e3d11eee1851ec6b73fd296ccd4a269bbda139f4b895e.png)" :question/worked-solution "We see from the graph that, approaching $x=-2$ from the right, the function approaches the value $y=4$. However, we cannot approach $x=-2$ from the left, because the function is not defined left of $x=-2.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-2^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,,\\quad\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-2^{+}}f(x)=4 \\,.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand,  approaching $x=2$ from the left, the function approaches the value $y=2$. However, we cannot approach $x=2$ from the right, because the function is not defined right of $x=2.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2^{-}}f(x)=2\\,,\\quad\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE} \\,.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Here, $\\text{DNE}$ is simply short for \"does not exist\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068912 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ffd5c800-ccb9-56a9-9fa0-86118664e9d6" "question-ddfb9440-a491-530c-8883-b3b8364d490f" "question-2e89b2a9-3a87-5f49-9315-671676fe1a51"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068635 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below? \n\nI. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^-}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$\n\nIII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)$\n\n![](images/1c/1c94c7d99009c46023556ce0f4f662f5a90ad315048099394016390552ade809.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the limits in question.\n\nLooking at the graph, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ However, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined. So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^-}f(x)=0, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\text{DNE} \\, .\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined, and as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)=\\text{DNE}, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)=0 \\, .\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\nI. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^-}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$ becomes $0=0,$ which is true.\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$ becomes $\\text{DNE}=0,$ which is false.\n\nIII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)$ becomes $\\text{DNE}=\\text{DNE},$ which is false because we can't compare the undefined values.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-2100ce73-4770-5a89-a8b5-3f4b9726facd" :question/id #uuid "2100ce73-4770-5a89-a8b5-3f4b9726facd" :question/math-academy-id "q-342097" :question/problem "![](images/78/781d755a292a18934af5b28436593262070527aa41da3bda092f979d388ef1f7.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=$  \n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=$  \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-2$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ee1f9f9d-34b1-5294-af05-80a2cd312768" "answer-field-0f94b681-420a-5f84-b8ee-e7d6ec657549"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ee1f9f9d-34b1-5294-af05-80a2cd312768" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee1f9f9d-34b1-5294-af05-80a2cd312768" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dbe3cfc7-17ac-5090-b863-c19ac950e9ee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dbe3cfc7-17ac-5090-b863-c19ac950e9ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbe3cfc7-17ac-5090-b863-c19ac950e9ee" :answer/id #uuid "dbe3cfc7-17ac-5090-b863-c19ac950e9ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0f94b681-420a-5f84-b8ee-e7d6ec657549" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f94b681-420a-5f84-b8ee-e7d6ec657549" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2fddcb65-0b3f-5376-bb41-38935b6ad19f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fddcb65-0b3f-5376-bb41-38935b6ad19f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fddcb65-0b3f-5376-bb41-38935b6ad19f" :answer/id #uuid "2fddcb65-0b3f-5376-bb41-38935b6ad19f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-585630ea-edc2-5584-9703-3115d4a68d23" :question/id #uuid "585630ea-edc2-5584-9703-3115d4a68d23" :question/math-academy-id "q-342118" :question/problem "![](images/44/441d472f60a9cc7415799032f66d2a618bf8419336f52aefb47f3b1ae7640875.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $3$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $3$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1fffec79-9b7c-5465-b9d1-a4732de4ab53" "answer-field-83f8be5a-aa05-5525-aa41-66f7fec68c54"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1fffec79-9b7c-5465-b9d1-a4732de4ab53" :answer-field/id #uuid "1fffec79-9b7c-5465-b9d1-a4732de4ab53" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-53d320bf-671c-5132-aad9-aed99b12b41d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-53d320bf-671c-5132-aad9-aed99b12b41d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53d320bf-671c-5132-aad9-aed99b12b41d" :answer/id #uuid "53d320bf-671c-5132-aad9-aed99b12b41d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-83f8be5a-aa05-5525-aa41-66f7fec68c54" :answer-field/id #uuid "83f8be5a-aa05-5525-aa41-66f7fec68c54" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cf35967a-ba19-53d4-bf98-6f45147add4d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cf35967a-ba19-53d4-bf98-6f45147add4d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf35967a-ba19-53d4-bf98-6f45147add4d" :answer/id #uuid "cf35967a-ba19-53d4-bf98-6f45147add4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f85c0d08-ef45-5aca-a806-01e365550a2b" :question/id #uuid "f85c0d08-ef45-5aca-a806-01e365550a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106429" :question/problem "![](images/52/52478c6cdcae0ba1e46b34eea8a4115f2f5f14d8973604c5ab589842bb7db262.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ (shown above) is defined on $x\\in [-4,1).$ Find the right-sided limit at $x=-4$ and the left-sided limit at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-4$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1\\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5c34f4e5-750e-5d27-a487-4438e118c498"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5c34f4e5-750e-5d27-a487-4438e118c498" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c34f4e5-750e-5d27-a487-4438e118c498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3f63906b-d536-5800-9221-6a3236936f33" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c6d40e96-d5e2-59e3-9710-e58888103289" "answer-3f63906b-d536-5800-9221-6a3236936f33" "answer-eb8b2ee6-4859-523e-9aba-6da0a4144bfc" "answer-bb40da77-9493-57f8-81ff-407b3b027a6c" "answer-6d6007a9-0a8d-51bb-aa96-f88a2935158e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6d40e96-d5e2-59e3-9710-e58888103289" :answer/id #uuid "c6d40e96-d5e2-59e3-9710-e58888103289" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f63906b-d536-5800-9221-6a3236936f33" :answer/id #uuid "3f63906b-d536-5800-9221-6a3236936f33" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb8b2ee6-4859-523e-9aba-6da0a4144bfc" :answer/id #uuid "eb8b2ee6-4859-523e-9aba-6da0a4144bfc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb40da77-9493-57f8-81ff-407b3b027a6c" :answer/id #uuid "bb40da77-9493-57f8-81ff-407b3b027a6c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d6007a9-0a8d-51bb-aa96-f88a2935158e" :answer/id #uuid "6d6007a9-0a8d-51bb-aa96-f88a2935158e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c6b36716-81d8-575e-98da-f48320938f53" :question/id #uuid "c6b36716-81d8-575e-98da-f48320938f53" :question/math-academy-id "q-342137" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd7ee98596021320d0f24d5b813de476ffabaa48156316091e5f23fed9e20d30.png)\n\nThe function $y=g(x)$ (shown above) is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find the left-sided limit at $x=-1$ and the right-sided limit at $x=2.$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d061e6e3-b59b-572b-aa97-b05d15013910" "answer-field-e6785073-50ec-528b-8583-c2326c8a4528"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d061e6e3-b59b-572b-aa97-b05d15013910" :answer-field/id #uuid "d061e6e3-b59b-572b-aa97-b05d15013910" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1cc4a6d9-dfe0-5f37-99cb-23132a2d44d9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1cc4a6d9-dfe0-5f37-99cb-23132a2d44d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1cc4a6d9-dfe0-5f37-99cb-23132a2d44d9" :answer/id #uuid "1cc4a6d9-dfe0-5f37-99cb-23132a2d44d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e6785073-50ec-528b-8583-c2326c8a4528" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6785073-50ec-528b-8583-c2326c8a4528" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d7342b3f-0153-5cba-b7a5-6621f41aac43" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d7342b3f-0153-5cba-b7a5-6621f41aac43"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7342b3f-0153-5cba-b7a5-6621f41aac43" :answer/id #uuid "d7342b3f-0153-5cba-b7a5-6621f41aac43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ffd5c800-ccb9-56a9-9fa0-86118664e9d6" :question/id #uuid "ffd5c800-ccb9-56a9-9fa0-86118664e9d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-34846" :question/problem "![](images/11/11e1c9a550944fc8435fc0d6f17be7d172e0c6d8f82fc1af950896f6ec8176b6.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?  \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=1$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$  approaches $y=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1$, Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined. Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nInstead, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-08777f1a-cad8-551c-a333-cf1d993c4fdf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-08777f1a-cad8-551c-a333-cf1d993c4fdf" :answer-field/id #uuid "08777f1a-cad8-551c-a333-cf1d993c4fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6a7c8fad-19aa-53fe-9102-1f62a0707917" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e266eb35-fcda-5866-88e8-df50dcf09a67" "answer-e33bc3c9-1b04-56f0-b61b-ebf99ab2a3df" "answer-3f68545a-5789-5d26-b54a-b5c18869ee17" "answer-c326c467-211d-5b87-823b-764014a39998" "answer-6a7c8fad-19aa-53fe-9102-1f62a0707917"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e266eb35-fcda-5866-88e8-df50dcf09a67" :answer/id #uuid "e266eb35-fcda-5866-88e8-df50dcf09a67" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e33bc3c9-1b04-56f0-b61b-ebf99ab2a3df" :answer/id #uuid "e33bc3c9-1b04-56f0-b61b-ebf99ab2a3df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f68545a-5789-5d26-b54a-b5c18869ee17" :answer/id #uuid "3f68545a-5789-5d26-b54a-b5c18869ee17" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c326c467-211d-5b87-823b-764014a39998" :answer/id #uuid "c326c467-211d-5b87-823b-764014a39998" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a7c8fad-19aa-53fe-9102-1f62a0707917" :answer/id #uuid "6a7c8fad-19aa-53fe-9102-1f62a0707917" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ddfb9440-a491-530c-8883-b3b8364d490f" :question/id #uuid "ddfb9440-a491-530c-8883-b3b8364d490f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106434" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f01058e0460ac0de134f36ce8a83aada0a719565f78c640b713526ec1b1a3a99.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?  \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $-3$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=0,$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined, Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=-2$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=-2\\,.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=0$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1bf8286a-48fb-5ac5-864d-0bb3e1b4c15c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1bf8286a-48fb-5ac5-864d-0bb3e1b4c15c" :answer-field/id #uuid "1bf8286a-48fb-5ac5-864d-0bb3e1b4c15c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-373cc9b6-5ba1-5f89-8767-f6478dee4f74" :answer-field/choices ["answer-373cc9b6-5ba1-5f89-8767-f6478dee4f74" "answer-8ac0a9ca-fc4d-5f48-9ca4-aabb3f225399" "answer-bd849b07-b5b4-557f-9537-a17eaa8ba010" "answer-3350670e-0708-5a65-8760-c36b78ace901" "answer-25e5f3ac-6d0f-5113-bfc8-7212b2f51ce5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-373cc9b6-5ba1-5f89-8767-f6478dee4f74" :answer/id #uuid "373cc9b6-5ba1-5f89-8767-f6478dee4f74" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ac0a9ca-fc4d-5f48-9ca4-aabb3f225399" :answer/id #uuid "8ac0a9ca-fc4d-5f48-9ca4-aabb3f225399" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd849b07-b5b4-557f-9537-a17eaa8ba010" :answer/id #uuid "bd849b07-b5b4-557f-9537-a17eaa8ba010" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3350670e-0708-5a65-8760-c36b78ace901" :answer/id #uuid "3350670e-0708-5a65-8760-c36b78ace901" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25e5f3ac-6d0f-5113-bfc8-7212b2f51ce5" :answer/id #uuid "25e5f3ac-6d0f-5113-bfc8-7212b2f51ce5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2e89b2a9-3a87-5f49-9315-671676fe1a51" :question/id #uuid "2e89b2a9-3a87-5f49-9315-671676fe1a51" :question/math-academy-id "q-106457" :question/problem "![](images/21/21cf8b9a6a94900b68a565a2d0915ba849379a134009c37db64bd5ff1eea2ac7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the limits in question. \n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=-2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2\\,.\n$$\n\nWe cannot approach $x=1$ from the right because the function is not defined to the right of $x=1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we cannot approach $2$ from the left because the function is not defined to the left of $x=2.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- I. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)$ becomes $2=-2,$ which is false.\n\n- II. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is false because neither one-sided limit exists.\n\n- III. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ becomes $1=1,$ which is true.\n\nThus, only statement III is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5692066-4d1c-50f0-b011-92ff8c2f668e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5692066-4d1c-50f0-b011-92ff8c2f668e" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5692066-4d1c-50f0-b011-92ff8c2f668e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-55f9cd12-5bf6-589a-87b8-08c39a5e9f25" :answer-field/choices ["answer-67a628cb-3f24-511c-9ee5-fd6644c9f051" "answer-7b30b91a-fe7c-5fd1-8d01-b6cbe5bb70eb" "answer-06f6bdb5-79f3-5acf-9f2b-9230d9baf9d2" "answer-55f9cd12-5bf6-589a-87b8-08c39a5e9f25" "answer-64f08c7a-c573-5faf-aad5-ddf460636e59"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67a628cb-3f24-511c-9ee5-fd6644c9f051" :answer/id #uuid "67a628cb-3f24-511c-9ee5-fd6644c9f051" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b30b91a-fe7c-5fd1-8d01-b6cbe5bb70eb" :answer/id #uuid "7b30b91a-fe7c-5fd1-8d01-b6cbe5bb70eb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06f6bdb5-79f3-5acf-9f2b-9230d9baf9d2" :answer/id #uuid "06f6bdb5-79f3-5acf-9f2b-9230d9baf9d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55f9cd12-5bf6-589a-87b8-08c39a5e9f25" :answer/id #uuid "55f9cd12-5bf6-589a-87b8-08c39a5e9f25" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64f08c7a-c573-5faf-aad5-ddf460636e59" :answer/id #uuid "64f08c7a-c573-5faf-aad5-ddf460636e59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069933 :tutorial/content "We already know that $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, a} f(x)$ represents the limit of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $a.$ However, there are really two ways that $x$ can approach $a$ -- from the left side or from the right side -- and sometimes, it matters which direction we choose.\n\nFor example, consider the function below.\n\n- As $x \\to -1$ from the left, we have $f(x) \\to -4,$ but\n\n- as $x \\to -1$ from the right, we have $f(x) \\to -3.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7a08cf136b1fdb33973c4f94300b08d18ca89fae8c2f9016155d05e21f4d610.png)\n\nTo be more precise about the direction in which $x$ approaches $a$, we use superscripts. The minus sign $(-)$ means \"from the left\" and the plus sign $(+)$ means \"from the right.\"\n\n- **Left-sided limit:** $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, a^-} f(x)$ represents the limit of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $a$ *from the left.*\n\n- **Right-sided limit:** $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, a^+} f(x)$ represents the limit of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $a$ *from the right.*\n\nFor the function graphed above, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1^{-}}f(x)=-4\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1^{+}}f(x)=-3 \\,.\n$$\n\nAt the same time, if we consider the point $x=-5,$ we note that the left-sided limit at this point does not exist because the function to the left of $x=-5$ is not defined. If the limit **does not exist**, we usually denote it using the abbreviation $\\textrm{DNE}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-5^{-}}f(x)=\\textrm{DNE}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The \"minus\" superscript $(-)$ indicates \"left\" because the left direction is the negative direction on the number line, and the \"plus\" superscript $(+)$ indicates \"right\" because the right direction is the positive direction on the number line." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069957 :tutorial/content "If two limits *do not exist*, then we *cannot* say that they are equal because we can't compare the undefined values.\n\nSo, if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x) = \\text{DNE}, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x \\to b} g(x) = \\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)  \\neq  \\lim\\limits_{x \\to b} g(x) \\, .\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068996 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186095826 :answer-field/choices 17592186095827] [:db/retract 17592186095826 :answer-field/choices 17592186095828] [:db/retract 17592186095826 :answer-field/choices 17592186095829] [:db/retract 17592186095826 :answer-field/choices 17592186095830] [:db/retract 17592186095826 :answer-field/choices 17592186095831] [:db/retract 17592186093854 :answer-field/choices 17592186093856] [:db/retract 17592186093854 :answer-field/choices 17592186093857] [:db/retract 17592186093854 :answer-field/choices 17592186093858] [:db/retract 17592186093854 :answer-field/choices 17592186093859] [:db/retract 17592186093854 :answer-field/choices 17592186093855] {:db/id 17592186096820 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3d9e0c2d-cfb3-5637-bf5c-58e8b1c970c9" "question-c81fea43-7a0e-5b24-b7d9-362bddbf7de1" "question-948d9306-7c7c-59c6-bfff-2f8bea699654" "question-b5e88433-85a5-5481-9536-7dfae4f91322"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096562 :question/problem "Let $f(x)=e^{x}$ and let $P_2(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when $P_2(x)$ is used to approximate $e^{1.1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error $R_2$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial $P_2$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|R_2| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}|x-1|^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|f^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=1.1.$ Plugging $x=1.1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|1.1-1{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.1{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|f^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,1.1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|{e}^{x}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|f^{(3)}(x)|=e^x$ is an increasing function on the interval $[1,1.1],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.1.$ So\n\n$$\nM = |f^{(3)}(1.1)| =e^{1.1},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|R_2| \\leq \\dfrac{e^{1.1}}{6}|0.1|^{3}   \\approx 0.000\\,501,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a6eeece9-3543-5575-94c0-d71f28347d32" "question-009bfd07-1f0d-5688-8e73-36cc8b112d6e" "question-e46215f5-878f-5a8e-bbfe-fcdc781327b3" "question-fc2772cd-2bc4-5ed7-b988-82cf9e52af83"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096563 :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\ln(1-x)$ and let $P_3(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when $P_3(x)$ is used to approximate $\\ln(0.9)$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error $R_3$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial $P_3$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|R_3| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{4!}|x-0|^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|f^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.1.$ Plugging $x=0.1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|0.1-0{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|0.1{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|f^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\,\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-\\,\\frac{6}{(x-1)^{4}} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|-\\,\\frac{6}{(x-1)^{4}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(x-1)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|f^{(4)}(x)|=\\dfrac{6}{(x-1)^4}$ is an increasing function on the interval $[0,0.1],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.1.$ So\n\n$$\nM = |f^{(4)}(0.1)| =\\dfrac{6}{(0.1-1)^4} = \\dfrac{6}{(0.9)^4},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|R_3| \\leq \\dfrac{ \\left( \\dfrac{6}{(0.9)^4} \\right) }{24}|0.1|^{4}  \\approx 0.000\\,038,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-3d9e0c2d-cfb3-5637-bf5c-58e8b1c970c9" :question/id #uuid "3d9e0c2d-cfb3-5637-bf5c-58e8b1c970c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103872" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-1{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\nPlugging $x=2$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|2-1{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|1{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,2].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-\\frac{15}{8}{x}^{-7/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}|=\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[1,2],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(1)|=\\frac{15}{8(1)^{7/2}}=\\frac{15}{8},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{15}{8})}{6}|1{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16} \\\\  & ≈0.313\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nup to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4166e2b7-c9ea-52ad-b2a3-8d0fb32213b3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4166e2b7-c9ea-52ad-b2a3-8d0fb32213b3" :answer-field/id #uuid "4166e2b7-c9ea-52ad-b2a3-8d0fb32213b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-99d6488f-dc3c-50ed-ba38-298b99e1c11a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2bbe50e9-7976-59a7-89c3-05577c4d4e69" "answer-40a27b19-7d0a-5731-a5f5-80e7d90ea7b6" "answer-79fa3435-6ee8-5d6f-8f2d-f21bcdc60b02" "answer-adaee6c3-bba1-5f68-9ad5-e21e92e1228b" "answer-99d6488f-dc3c-50ed-ba38-298b99e1c11a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bbe50e9-7976-59a7-89c3-05577c4d4e69" :answer/id #uuid "2bbe50e9-7976-59a7-89c3-05577c4d4e69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.012" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40a27b19-7d0a-5731-a5f5-80e7d90ea7b6" :answer/id #uuid "40a27b19-7d0a-5731-a5f5-80e7d90ea7b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.031" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79fa3435-6ee8-5d6f-8f2d-f21bcdc60b02" :answer/id #uuid "79fa3435-6ee8-5d6f-8f2d-f21bcdc60b02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.123" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adaee6c3-bba1-5f68-9ad5-e21e92e1228b" :answer/id #uuid "adaee6c3-bba1-5f68-9ad5-e21e92e1228b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.215" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99d6488f-dc3c-50ed-ba38-298b99e1c11a" :answer/id #uuid "99d6488f-dc3c-50ed-ba38-298b99e1c11a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.313" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c81fea43-7a0e-5b24-b7d9-362bddbf7de1" :question/id #uuid "c81fea43-7a0e-5b24-b7d9-362bddbf7de1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81409" :question/problem "Let $f(x)={e}^{-x}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}={e}^{-0.5}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-0{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.5.$ \n\nPlugging $x=0.5$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|0.5-0{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.5{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.5].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-{e}^{-x}|={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|={e}^{-x}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,0.5],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(0)|=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{1}{6}|0.5{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot {2}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-26eade32-ac9c-595f-b7c2-c1d810c396a7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-26eade32-ac9c-595f-b7c2-c1d810c396a7" :answer-field/id #uuid "26eade32-ac9c-595f-b7c2-c1d810c396a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-556f0cd0-d7c9-560a-a9b5-86a425bea067" :answer-field/choices ["answer-64e0788d-8a4e-5cb8-aa06-7cbe63186d8e" "answer-556f0cd0-d7c9-560a-a9b5-86a425bea067" "answer-13f33b43-4368-59b6-9c7d-d1d7b630d1f9" "answer-3d98ce0f-129f-51bf-9a3e-c9f63afae9e3" "answer-ddec7804-92b7-519d-b753-624b291a0137"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64e0788d-8a4e-5cb8-aa06-7cbe63186d8e" :answer/id #uuid "64e0788d-8a4e-5cb8-aa06-7cbe63186d8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-556f0cd0-d7c9-560a-a9b5-86a425bea067" :answer/id #uuid "556f0cd0-d7c9-560a-a9b5-86a425bea067" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{48}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13f33b43-4368-59b6-9c7d-d1d7b630d1f9" :answer/id #uuid "13f33b43-4368-59b6-9c7d-d1d7b630d1f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d98ce0f-129f-51bf-9a3e-c9f63afae9e3" :answer/id #uuid "3d98ce0f-129f-51bf-9a3e-c9f63afae9e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddec7804-92b7-519d-b753-624b291a0137" :answer/id #uuid "ddec7804-92b7-519d-b753-624b291a0137" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{48}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-948d9306-7c7c-59c6-bfff-2f8bea699654" :question/id #uuid "948d9306-7c7c-59c6-bfff-2f8bea699654" :question/math-academy-id "q-103856" :question/problem "Let $f(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate ${e}^{-3.3}={e}^{-3(1.1)}.$ Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-1{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=1.1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=1.1$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|1.1-1{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.1{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-3{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-27{e}^{-3x} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-27{e}^{-3x}|=27{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=27{e}^{-3x}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[1,1.1],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(1)|=27{e}^{-3(1)}=\\frac{27}{{e}^{3}},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{27}{{e}^{3}})}{6}|0.1{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2{e}^{3}}\\cdot 0.001 \\\\  & ≈0.0002.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-505704bc-04a4-5876-b503-4f2abf719102"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-505704bc-04a4-5876-b503-4f2abf719102" :answer-field/id #uuid "505704bc-04a4-5876-b503-4f2abf719102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-49e21de5-b7bb-503a-8a7b-68e62683ea89" :answer-field/choices ["answer-49e21de5-b7bb-503a-8a7b-68e62683ea89" "answer-5fee810e-caf1-5778-8d50-ddcea2fbc768" "answer-d11dc14d-c3d7-598f-81c0-4c080c11ce7d" "answer-60fb0da3-26a9-54ba-8f00-0b915d7c609f" "answer-e5d65d11-05f8-529f-a9a0-2faa772f8a7a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49e21de5-b7bb-503a-8a7b-68e62683ea89" :answer/id #uuid "49e21de5-b7bb-503a-8a7b-68e62683ea89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.0002" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fee810e-caf1-5778-8d50-ddcea2fbc768" :answer/id #uuid "5fee810e-caf1-5778-8d50-ddcea2fbc768" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.0006" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d11dc14d-c3d7-598f-81c0-4c080c11ce7d" :answer/id #uuid "d11dc14d-c3d7-598f-81c0-4c080c11ce7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.0018" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60fb0da3-26a9-54ba-8f00-0b915d7c609f" :answer/id #uuid "60fb0da3-26a9-54ba-8f00-0b915d7c609f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.0105" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5d65d11-05f8-529f-a9a0-2faa772f8a7a" :answer/id #uuid "e5d65d11-05f8-529f-a9a0-2faa772f8a7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.0114" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b5e88433-85a5-5481-9536-7dfae4f91322" :question/id #uuid "b5e88433-85a5-5481-9536-7dfae4f91322" :question/math-academy-id "q-103852" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=(1+x)^{3/2}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $(1.9)^{3/2}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-0{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.9.$ \n\nPlugging $x=0.9$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|0.9-0{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.9{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.9].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1+x)^{3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}(1+x)^{1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}(1+x)^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{8}(1+x)^{-3/2} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{3}{8}(1+x)^{-3/2}|=\\frac{3}{8(1+x)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{3}{8(1+x)^{3/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,0.9],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(0)|=\\frac{3}{8(1+0)^{3/2}}=\\frac{3}{8},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{3}{8})}{6}|0.9{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.9)^{3}}{16} \\\\  & ≈0.046.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f20a2fb-0d1a-5ecb-8bf0-40ff423fe5fc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f20a2fb-0d1a-5ecb-8bf0-40ff423fe5fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f20a2fb-0d1a-5ecb-8bf0-40ff423fe5fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-380cc8e8-a9b5-54c3-abf5-c67aa237c154" :answer-field/choices ["answer-82ebe979-ce57-57ec-99a9-a11aee8474c0" "answer-221295e3-b7a7-5e11-a8d8-39994098832c" "answer-e3af7625-c031-5f9a-8448-5e0736d3e728" "answer-380cc8e8-a9b5-54c3-abf5-c67aa237c154" "answer-198af1c0-5c8f-56ac-b8d0-73fd97e93b21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82ebe979-ce57-57ec-99a9-a11aee8474c0" :answer/id #uuid "82ebe979-ce57-57ec-99a9-a11aee8474c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.009" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-221295e3-b7a7-5e11-a8d8-39994098832c" :answer/id #uuid "221295e3-b7a7-5e11-a8d8-39994098832c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.017" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3af7625-c031-5f9a-8448-5e0736d3e728" :answer/id #uuid "e3af7625-c031-5f9a-8448-5e0736d3e728" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.024" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-380cc8e8-a9b5-54c3-abf5-c67aa237c154" :answer/id #uuid "380cc8e8-a9b5-54c3-abf5-c67aa237c154" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.046" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-198af1c0-5c8f-56ac-b8d0-73fd97e93b21" :answer/id #uuid "198af1c0-5c8f-56ac-b8d0-73fd97e93b21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le 0.052" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097317 :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=2.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.3.$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-2{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=2$ and $x=2.3.$ \n\nPlugging $x=2.3$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}\\,{|2.3-2|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}\\,{|0.3|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[2,2.3].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}|=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[2,2.3],$ its value is greatest at $x=2.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(2)|=\\frac{2}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{6}\\,{|0.3|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{0.3}^{3}}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8\\,000}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186095826 :answer-field/correct "answer-122de8ac-c699-548f-adbb-e4671112f9d3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe19f1ce-1baf-5ea3-a712-8dedf9961f1b" "answer-548b7b40-9324-5d50-a272-34d07facd245" "answer-58e49fdf-507c-5cd4-9d51-1bf8b6e8b879" "answer-3c49d6b0-1cee-5345-9267-3413b034af35" "answer-122de8ac-c699-548f-adbb-e4671112f9d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe19f1ce-1baf-5ea3-a712-8dedf9961f1b" :answer/id #uuid "fe19f1ce-1baf-5ea3-a712-8dedf9961f1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{27}{400}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-548b7b40-9324-5d50-a272-34d07facd245" :answer/id #uuid "548b7b40-9324-5d50-a272-34d07facd245" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{3}{11\\,000}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58e49fdf-507c-5cd4-9d51-1bf8b6e8b879" :answer/id #uuid "58e49fdf-507c-5cd4-9d51-1bf8b6e8b879" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{3}{800}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c49d6b0-1cee-5345-9267-3413b034af35" :answer/id #uuid "3c49d6b0-1cee-5345-9267-3413b034af35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{9}{400}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-122de8ac-c699-548f-adbb-e4671112f9d3" :answer/id #uuid "122de8ac-c699-548f-adbb-e4671112f9d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{9}{8\\,000}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a6eeece9-3543-5575-94c0-d71f28347d32" :question/id #uuid "a6eeece9-3543-5575-94c0-d71f28347d32" :question/math-academy-id "q-13972" :question/problem "Use the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{-x}$ about $x=0$ to approximate the value of the number $\\frac{1}{e}.$ Find the Lagrange error bound for this approximation." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-0{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=1$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|1-0{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|1{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|{e}^{-x}|={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|={e}^{-x}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,1],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(0)|={e}^{-0}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{24}|1{|}^{4}=\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-969e58d0-648c-5c59-91c2-cc3ac39b94f1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-969e58d0-648c-5c59-91c2-cc3ac39b94f1" :answer-field/id #uuid "969e58d0-648c-5c59-91c2-cc3ac39b94f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-39764ab5-f63e-5d48-b7eb-d836de9a7e06" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45c01446-829f-5f53-b773-5a46add2a345" "answer-39764ab5-f63e-5d48-b7eb-d836de9a7e06" "answer-add5f89b-e7d4-5720-9518-dfe4679a52c2" "answer-f610a192-d5e1-5235-b2a1-ce3249d5f8c3" "answer-a9779fc8-c256-59bd-b94b-25cb9d4c9cc9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45c01446-829f-5f53-b773-5a46add2a345" :answer/id #uuid "45c01446-829f-5f53-b773-5a46add2a345" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{120}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39764ab5-f63e-5d48-b7eb-d836de9a7e06" :answer/id #uuid "39764ab5-f63e-5d48-b7eb-d836de9a7e06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{24}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-add5f89b-e7d4-5720-9518-dfe4679a52c2" :answer/id #uuid "add5f89b-e7d4-5720-9518-dfe4679a52c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f610a192-d5e1-5235-b2a1-ce3249d5f8c3" :answer/id #uuid "f610a192-d5e1-5235-b2a1-ce3249d5f8c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{e}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9779fc8-c256-59bd-b94b-25cb9d4c9cc9" :answer/id #uuid "a9779fc8-c256-59bd-b94b-25cb9d4c9cc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{e}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-009bfd07-1f0d-5688-8e73-36cc8b112d6e" :question/id #uuid "009bfd07-1f0d-5688-8e73-36cc8b112d6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-103882" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-1{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=3.$ \n\nPlugging $x=3$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|3-1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|2{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,3].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2}|=\\frac{15}{16}{x}^{-7/2}=\\frac{15}{16{x}^{7/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|=\\frac{15}{16{x}^{7/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[1,3],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(1)|=\\frac{15}{16(1)^{7/2}}=\\frac{15}{16},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{15}{16})}{24}|2{|}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot {2}^{4}}{16\\cdot 24} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-287ab9b4-ef99-51aa-b119-51a1269f75d5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-287ab9b4-ef99-51aa-b119-51a1269f75d5" :answer-field/id #uuid "287ab9b4-ef99-51aa-b119-51a1269f75d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-17aeea7d-8357-5924-a3be-cd6d28b250ee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ae8d020f-a077-5090-baab-35bc37c195f3" "answer-028fcae0-cbd2-5656-994f-f49bd8d68fde" "answer-11c234cf-17eb-5252-9439-261d6cb33e3f" "answer-2c6c9f8d-9261-5310-bdc5-6b757360cea7" "answer-17aeea7d-8357-5924-a3be-cd6d28b250ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae8d020f-a077-5090-baab-35bc37c195f3" :answer/id #uuid "ae8d020f-a077-5090-baab-35bc37c195f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-028fcae0-cbd2-5656-994f-f49bd8d68fde" :answer/id #uuid "028fcae0-cbd2-5656-994f-f49bd8d68fde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11c234cf-17eb-5252-9439-261d6cb33e3f" :answer/id #uuid "11c234cf-17eb-5252-9439-261d6cb33e3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c6c9f8d-9261-5310-bdc5-6b757360cea7" :answer/id #uuid "2c6c9f8d-9261-5310-bdc5-6b757360cea7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17aeea7d-8357-5924-a3be-cd6d28b250ee" :answer/id #uuid "17aeea7d-8357-5924-a3be-cd6d28b250ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{5}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e46215f5-878f-5a8e-bbfe-fcdc781327b3" :question/id #uuid "e46215f5-878f-5a8e-bbfe-fcdc781327b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81411" :question/problem "Let $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{e}}={e}^{-1/3}.$ Round your answer to $5$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-0{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $a=0.$ \n\nPlugging $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}{|-\\frac{1}{3}-0|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}{|-\\frac{1}{3}|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[-\\frac{1}{3},0].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|{e}^{x}|={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|={e}^{x}$ is an increasing function on the interval $[-\\frac{1}{3},0],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(0)|={e}^{0}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{1}{24}{|-\\frac{1}{3}|}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24\\cdot {3}^{4}} \\\\  & ≈0.000\\,51,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ec1a6f23-dffc-5c92-b6f1-97a12771feda"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec1a6f23-dffc-5c92-b6f1-97a12771feda" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec1a6f23-dffc-5c92-b6f1-97a12771feda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b1b6c2c0-6a4f-5fc5-854b-81d672de6117" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a1474263-af15-566e-8a13-b2427579c1f4" "answer-e0fa8715-79f1-5f77-accf-e8e0b0e15fcf" "answer-67dee9ee-b688-5a6c-85b3-9f9d1783148a" "answer-b1b6c2c0-6a4f-5fc5-854b-81d672de6117" "answer-61926743-8db2-5522-a575-4884614fd77d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1474263-af15-566e-8a13-b2427579c1f4" :answer/id #uuid "a1474263-af15-566e-8a13-b2427579c1f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,34" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0fa8715-79f1-5f77-accf-e8e0b0e15fcf" :answer/id #uuid "e0fa8715-79f1-5f77-accf-e8e0b0e15fcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,39" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67dee9ee-b688-5a6c-85b3-9f9d1783148a" :answer/id #uuid "67dee9ee-b688-5a6c-85b3-9f9d1783148a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,43" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1b6c2c0-6a4f-5fc5-854b-81d672de6117" :answer/id #uuid "b1b6c2c0-6a4f-5fc5-854b-81d672de6117" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,51" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61926743-8db2-5522-a575-4884614fd77d" :answer/id #uuid "61926743-8db2-5522-a575-4884614fd77d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,57" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096980 :question/problem "Let $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate ${e}^{0.65}.$ Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-1{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $x=0.65$ and $a=1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=0.65$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|0.65-1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|-0.35{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0.65,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|{e}^{x}|={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|={e}^{x}$ is an increasing function on the interval $[0.65,1],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(1)|={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{e}{24}|-0.35{|}^{4}≈0.001\\,7,\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093854 :answer-field/correct "answer-b6100635-bfad-58ed-a622-3094a1b967a6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2ff2cb8-a71a-56c9-ba60-836e6a71e4be" "answer-b6100635-bfad-58ed-a622-3094a1b967a6" "answer-a73028cb-a36c-5800-a191-1ab9cada333b" "answer-540451fd-fe97-5678-bf5f-389fd52f9b5e" "answer-c8762886-22b3-5e2b-9614-762f57eb3d22"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2ff2cb8-a71a-56c9-ba60-836e6a71e4be" :answer/id #uuid "d2ff2cb8-a71a-56c9-ba60-836e6a71e4be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.000\\,2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6100635-bfad-58ed-a622-3094a1b967a6" :answer/id #uuid "b6100635-bfad-58ed-a622-3094a1b967a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.001\\,7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a73028cb-a36c-5800-a191-1ab9cada333b" :answer/id #uuid "a73028cb-a36c-5800-a191-1ab9cada333b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.002\\,4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-540451fd-fe97-5678-bf5f-389fd52f9b5e" :answer/id #uuid "540451fd-fe97-5678-bf5f-389fd52f9b5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.003\\,2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8762886-22b3-5e2b-9614-762f57eb3d22" :answer/id #uuid "c8762886-22b3-5e2b-9614-762f57eb3d22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le 0.004\\,3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fc2772cd-2bc4-5ed7-b988-82cf9e52af83" :question/id #uuid "fc2772cd-2bc4-5ed7-b988-82cf9e52af83" :question/math-academy-id "q-103900" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1).$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-0{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=1$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|1-0{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|1{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,1],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(0)|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{24}|1{|}^{4}=\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eedf09a2-2732-57b4-bde6-4d08d850c1fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eedf09a2-2732-57b4-bde6-4d08d850c1fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "eedf09a2-2732-57b4-bde6-4d08d850c1fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1b5b14b6-a5c8-5b0e-9e69-f52d069588fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eea68c41-0fda-5c95-b448-41701dba855a" "answer-1b5b14b6-a5c8-5b0e-9e69-f52d069588fc" "answer-9dca8394-0fd7-5eab-89fc-e1babcf985e4" "answer-9688ddeb-1dfd-5f91-9a17-85eb71116616" "answer-b67fece6-0ac9-535e-8151-8eeb845f93a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eea68c41-0fda-5c95-b448-41701dba855a" :answer/id #uuid "eea68c41-0fda-5c95-b448-41701dba855a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b5b14b6-a5c8-5b0e-9e69-f52d069588fc" :answer/id #uuid "1b5b14b6-a5c8-5b0e-9e69-f52d069588fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{1}{24}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dca8394-0fd7-5eab-89fc-e1babcf985e4" :answer/id #uuid "9dca8394-0fd7-5eab-89fc-e1babcf985e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9688ddeb-1dfd-5f91-9a17-85eb71116616" :answer/id #uuid "9688ddeb-1dfd-5f91-9a17-85eb71116616" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{3}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b67fece6-0ac9-535e-8151-8eeb845f93a1" :answer/id #uuid "b67fece6-0ac9-535e-8151-8eeb845f93a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{3}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097803 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to *approximate* $f(x) = e^x$ for some value of $x.$ We can do this using the second-degree Taylor polynomial about a point $x=a.$ For example, the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $e^x$ about the point $x=1$ is shown below:\n\n$$\ne^x \\approx P_2(x) = e + e(x-1) + \\dfrac 1 2 e(x-1)^2\n$$\n\nIn general, the polynomial $P_2(x)$ is only an approximation to the function $f(x),$ which means that there will usually be some error whenever we use $P_2$ to approximate $f.$ We want to know how bad this error can get. Can we find an upper bound for the error? \n\nLet $R_2(x)$ be the **error** (or **remainder**) that we get when we use $P_2$ to approximate $f(x)=e^x,$ so \n\n$$\nR_2(x) = e^x  - P_2(x).\n$$\n\nThe **Lagrange error bound** states that an upper bound for $R_2$ is given by,\n\n$$\n|R_2| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}|x-a|^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ the maximum value of $|f^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a$ and $x.$ \n\nIn our case, since our Taylor polynomial is taken about the point $a=1,$ the Lagrange error bound becomes\n\n$$\n|R_2| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}|x-1|^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the Lagrange error bound looks a lot like the last term of the *next* Taylor polynomial, $P_3(x).$\n\nThe tricky part is finding the value of $M.$ We'll learn how to do that in the following example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097804 :tutorial/content "The Lagrange error bound that we've discussed so far is valid only for the error associated with a second-degree Taylor polynomial. But we can generalize the result to higher-order Taylor polynomials.\n\nLet $R_n(x)$ be the error involved in approximating a function $f(x)$ by its $n$th-degree Taylor polynomial $P_n(x)$ about $x=a.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nR_n(x) = f(x) - P_n(x).\n$$\n\nThen the Lagrange error bound for $R_n$ is given by\n\n$$\n|R_n(x)| \\leq \\dfrac{M}{(n+1)!}|x-a|^{n+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|f^{(n+1)}|$ on the interval between $a$ and $x.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096869 :activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115265 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a52aedb3-cb6c-5591-aec7-501eba1e68dd" "question-c23af42c-b294-5f6c-af96-07b0ad73f00b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115094 :question/problem "The graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) is shown below. Sketch the graph of ![](images/92/928f63764afcdf6f3d6b2544ea46c4ea9766984bf6c419024b280a1144fa879f.svg)\n\n![](images/0d/0d6473127647437b8f45b6a29713710534c9d36b4cc8be544be4f0a0a91a51bd.png)" :question/worked-solution "To graph the function ![](images/85/85749f0cf7d11e38e0842d75182adae674cbc66a0ae756723093c3d0fc789c86.svg) we take the graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) and compress it by factor of ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) parallel to the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis. This has the effect of dividing the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinates of all points on ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) by ![](images/9f/9fc073d1e6ebde82eb949a875e40be00b9196e9b14473ee13fa47a26a98d8450.svg)\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/42/42b655b11d17c7c4f6afd8f33fc048193b6b101dde586c67a6a40635d82c629a.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115264 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ac83ae74-1a8c-543b-99e8-3815a1447e2b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115093 :question/worked-solution "To graph the function $y = f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x\\right),$ we take the graph of $y = f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ae/ae191c7f3af5c16113d28c20572eba4559b08145327cab651a49ca7d39991502.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115266 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6bebb2ef-00e1-59ff-ace6-82ab8feb5573" "question-c6ba2a58-fad3-5500-859d-8acd4ee3f5d0" "question-49ad3904-35d2-53e9-8532-90b9810959eb" "question-4862c2b5-a520-5c18-b89e-ce5de629850e" "question-218ab2ee-b004-5ca6-a364-899b69265503"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115095 :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(6,7)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Find a point that must lie on the graph of $y= f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}x\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y = f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}x\\right)$ represents a stretch of the function $y = f(x)$ by a factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(6,7)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $\\left(4\\cdot 6 , 7\\right) = (24,7),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y = f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}x\\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a52aedb3-cb6c-5591-aec7-501eba1e68dd" :question/id #uuid "a52aedb3-cb6c-5591-aec7-501eba1e68dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-111623" :question/problem "![](images/86/869e65cb2894403a5fe9f5e5f68e1c7882f8c86aff7596e557958e1f6cbbe348.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(3x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "To graph the function $y=f(3x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b84a3ca0b17949c07748e73235d026d696c199136e448b32639959036c5389a8.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/an