 it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/15/15b9b0530dbd4a193efb2ffd6d7719472c6aa641714298c4644ae47d167cf175.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186112111 :answer-field/correct 17592186112115 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-6bebb2ef-00e1-59ff-ace6-82ab8feb5573" :question/id #uuid "6bebb2ef-00e1-59ff-ace6-82ab8feb5573" :question/math-academy-id "q-249484" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(2,-5)$ lies on the curve $y=f(7x).$ Find the point $Q$ that must lie on $y=f(x).$\n\nThe coordinates of $Q$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)=f(\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (7x)),$ the function $y=f(x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(7x)$ by a factor of $7$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(7x)$ by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,-5)$ on $f(7x)$ is mapped to the point $(7\\cdot 2,-5)=(14,-5),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f9b7c184-2a67-5c73-afc6-a80fafa63c8c" "answer-field-57da7ef3-0c94-50c0-9233-711a1a474bca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f9b7c184-2a67-5c73-afc6-a80fafa63c8c" :answer-field/id #uuid "f9b7c184-2a67-5c73-afc6-a80fafa63c8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-30b7b33d-f8d9-53ae-b05e-74b05abed6e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-30b7b33d-f8d9-53ae-b05e-74b05abed6e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30b7b33d-f8d9-53ae-b05e-74b05abed6e7" :answer/id #uuid "30b7b33d-f8d9-53ae-b05e-74b05abed6e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-57da7ef3-0c94-50c0-9233-711a1a474bca" :answer-field/id #uuid "57da7ef3-0c94-50c0-9233-711a1a474bca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-31eec92c-4aee-5333-a1f2-267cf91c079f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-31eec92c-4aee-5333-a1f2-267cf91c079f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31eec92c-4aee-5333-a1f2-267cf91c079f" :answer/id #uuid "31eec92c-4aee-5333-a1f2-267cf91c079f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c6ba2a58-fad3-5500-859d-8acd4ee3f5d0" :question/id #uuid "c6ba2a58-fad3-5500-859d-8acd4ee3f5d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-249480" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(5,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Find the point $Q$ that must lie on $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x).$\n\nThe coordinates of $Q$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(5,3)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $(4\\cdot 5,3)=(20,3),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x)$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b351670c-7194-5c35-a8e5-250770b6c8e6" "answer-field-25670d29-e707-557b-851a-0b474254fad5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b351670c-7194-5c35-a8e5-250770b6c8e6" :answer-field/id #uuid "b351670c-7194-5c35-a8e5-250770b6c8e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dbb79cb9-583f-5c86-888e-10bd88765ba3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dbb79cb9-583f-5c86-888e-10bd88765ba3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbb79cb9-583f-5c86-888e-10bd88765ba3" :answer/id #uuid "dbb79cb9-583f-5c86-888e-10bd88765ba3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-25670d29-e707-557b-851a-0b474254fad5" :answer-field/id #uuid "25670d29-e707-557b-851a-0b474254fad5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9af2f624-c49e-5aad-b7ac-1c85f0c67913" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9af2f624-c49e-5aad-b7ac-1c85f0c67913"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9af2f624-c49e-5aad-b7ac-1c85f0c67913" :answer/id #uuid "9af2f624-c49e-5aad-b7ac-1c85f0c67913" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-49ad3904-35d2-53e9-8532-90b9810959eb" :question/id #uuid "49ad3904-35d2-53e9-8532-90b9810959eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-112072" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(18,-12)$ lies on the curve $y=f(\\frac{1}{6}x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)=f(6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}x)),$ the function $y=f(x)$ represents a compression of the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{6}x)$ by a factor of $6$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(\\frac{1}{6}x)$ by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(18,-12)$ on $f(\\frac{1}{6}x)$ is mapped to the point $(\\frac{18}{6},-12)=(3,-12),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f625ea60-5168-512e-81e7-57bda382ded5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f625ea60-5168-512e-81e7-57bda382ded5" :answer-field/id #uuid "f625ea60-5168-512e-81e7-57bda382ded5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e3f25442-78ed-5fde-9634-46beeeee6d93" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9bd45aac-f30d-5000-86fe-224eb9ba93fb" "answer-e2db458e-b50d-50d0-ab27-0cd2a5e9acde" "answer-e3f25442-78ed-5fde-9634-46beeeee6d93" "answer-cddadebd-c519-54de-beb5-8cd662529d0d" "answer-e9fe45cc-7ef4-5e8e-a2b3-796c2f924460"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bd45aac-f30d-5000-86fe-224eb9ba93fb" :answer/id #uuid "9bd45aac-f30d-5000-86fe-224eb9ba93fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(108,-12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2db458e-b50d-50d0-ab27-0cd2a5e9acde" :answer/id #uuid "e2db458e-b50d-50d0-ab27-0cd2a5e9acde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(108,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3f25442-78ed-5fde-9634-46beeeee6d93" :answer/id #uuid "e3f25442-78ed-5fde-9634-46beeeee6d93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cddadebd-c519-54de-beb5-8cd662529d0d" :answer/id #uuid "cddadebd-c519-54de-beb5-8cd662529d0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9fe45cc-7ef4-5e8e-a2b3-796c2f924460" :answer/id #uuid "e9fe45cc-7ef4-5e8e-a2b3-796c2f924460" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-72)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4862c2b5-a520-5c18-b89e-ce5de629850e" :question/id #uuid "4862c2b5-a520-5c18-b89e-ce5de629850e" :question/math-academy-id "q-249487" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-15,6)$ lies on the curve $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x).$ Find the point $Q$ that must lie on $y=f(x).$\n\nThe coordinates of $Q$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)=f(3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}x)),$ the function $y=f(x)$ represents a compression of the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-15,6)$ on $f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ is mapped to the point $(\\frac{-15}{3},6)=(-5,6),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bb4cdc89-f0b0-5651-9006-97d7c5f0bbed" "answer-field-30cb0c6b-7211-5811-94b8-3438d0ab648e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bb4cdc89-f0b0-5651-9006-97d7c5f0bbed" :answer-field/id #uuid "bb4cdc89-f0b0-5651-9006-97d7c5f0bbed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b4f1599b-6806-5734-b494-6d3d1686f10f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b4f1599b-6806-5734-b494-6d3d1686f10f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4f1599b-6806-5734-b494-6d3d1686f10f" :answer/id #uuid "b4f1599b-6806-5734-b494-6d3d1686f10f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-30cb0c6b-7211-5811-94b8-3438d0ab648e" :answer-field/id #uuid "30cb0c6b-7211-5811-94b8-3438d0ab648e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9ef32a63-bde3-5142-b050-b6ddd35472ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9ef32a63-bde3-5142-b050-b6ddd35472ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ef32a63-bde3-5142-b050-b6ddd35472ed" :answer/id #uuid "9ef32a63-bde3-5142-b050-b6ddd35472ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-218ab2ee-b004-5ca6-a364-899b69265503" :question/id #uuid "218ab2ee-b004-5ca6-a364-899b69265503" :question/math-academy-id "q-79435" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-9,6)$ lies on the curve $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)=f(3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}x)),$ the function $y=f(x)$ represents a compression of the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-9,6)$ on $f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ is mapped to the point $(\\frac{(-9)}{3},6)=(-3,6),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-256febee-d392-5d02-9fce-d60999ce9e89"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-256febee-d392-5d02-9fce-d60999ce9e89" :answer-field/id #uuid "256febee-d392-5d02-9fce-d60999ce9e89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-caea27c1-ce8b-55b3-9c98-8e1c2d4fe10e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ab4e075a-2c3d-5b91-ad1b-f0d083595868" "answer-e549a086-100d-5f15-8002-3a18e0fcd22e" "answer-caea27c1-ce8b-55b3-9c98-8e1c2d4fe10e" "answer-77d73de5-7c0f-52cc-95b6-ac7d8905c7aa" "answer-e9772aa5-0fc4-50a4-b4da-335660752240"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab4e075a-2c3d-5b91-ad1b-f0d083595868" :answer/id #uuid "ab4e075a-2c3d-5b91-ad1b-f0d083595868" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-27,6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e549a086-100d-5f15-8002-3a18e0fcd22e" :answer/id #uuid "e549a086-100d-5f15-8002-3a18e0fcd22e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caea27c1-ce8b-55b3-9c98-8e1c2d4fe10e" :answer/id #uuid "caea27c1-ce8b-55b3-9c98-8e1c2d4fe10e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77d73de5-7c0f-52cc-95b6-ac7d8905c7aa" :answer/id #uuid "77d73de5-7c0f-52cc-95b6-ac7d8905c7aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-9,18)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9772aa5-0fc4-50a4-b4da-335660752240" :answer/id #uuid "e9772aa5-0fc4-50a4-b4da-335660752240" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-9,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116506 :tutorial/content "Consider the function ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) whose graph is shown below. How can we plot the graph of ![](images/12/120fd26f32d53872d244163d5a4d63931d0f881b62f3f5f7a924a9c06255ba1d.svg)\n\n![](images/1a/1a11bef66d0529418dde00e5713ed5e98f53a948c8a60f83fd056fd9e2c18698.png)\n\nWe can use some points on the graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) to find the corresponding points on the graphs of ![](images/a1/a142ed820c2bdafb8c1b7969028c7f4533c66fa772a73954c46f6b5eb91a050f.svg)\n\nFor example, let's take the point ![](images/8f/8f231df171077c673ad3f7611cfd99a5fb2753448232eb64a5a160a42a21a122.svg) The fact that it lies on the graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) means ![](images/53/5327a1cd77a2b8abb05cd13fa3d2c65cdd0a7ff447afd71e682a0282118e886c.svg) Then, if ![](images/06/068f771676e51ca5f58fb024dd4635c8da494dc49bd43398585945ce0920a425.svg) we have\n![](images/03/033afdad9ff42ad24f2d0f4388e6065adea3608bf49116364b5c13833ded06cf.svg) \n\nThis means the point ![](images/b2/b2f8b859ae7e2b4fe083670dc262b473bd79b7b1ee50a60712ae5bc13bffb954.svg) that lies on ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) has a corresponding point ![](images/e2/e2e718c0eb1b8cf9581404873ddc1cc4512141d32e2fb42b70f516257ec31365.svg) that lies on ![](images/a1/a142ed820c2bdafb8c1b7969028c7f4533c66fa772a73954c46f6b5eb91a050f.svg)\n\nSo, for each point on the graph of ![](images/c9/c956e6cbfb2d94fa56a68a3173ec78f60fee33a9066c9c44ca4096d67d7b1cba.svg) there will be a point on the graph of ![](images/b9/b9b8d001be0277e544ff2d040e5b537ad8d7df49d33027a56021f4ba62497de3.svg) with the *same* ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-value and *one half* the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-value:\n\n![](images/66/66e2a4dfc65fc35cd4b812ea418df3976c6e23cc002babaadd1921745353de6b.svg)\n\nFinally, we can plot the points and draw the graph of ![](images/29/2994084d895116512b0d26509c9108d059fa2e73bbc4ea785e8535fce4a7f75b.svg)\n\n![](images/8c/8ce6a8fd398025bfb9cd535fbc16db06a2a219e0d2451cf6278d81d4ab5c264a.png)\n\nThe graph of ![](images/b9/b9b8d001be0277e544ff2d040e5b537ad8d7df49d33027a56021f4ba62497de3.svg) is the graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) **horizontally scaled** by a factor of ![](images/d7/d7cae033e26adc59eeceb95b5807505dd0242e42982f350c36209152864cf261.svg)\n\nEquivalently, we can say that the original graph is **compressed** by a scale factor of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)\n\nIn general, the transformation ![](images/45/45950fa47c3a14fa07312805dd06e2638388335b9781c6ba1c07c76d7a5b108c.svg) scales the graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) horizontally by a factor of ![](images/aa/aad37c78cd66cde5ccbba8aa66a6d18afcb0fd5b7de52471203a690e0645e4c5.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/8d/8dc6f7e80ecaac9fa07890ce1881dd51de319597ae8b1dce821aa03c1f3eff06.svg) is greater than ![](images/76/76543f4805f22715457d55feb2a3c87d0d873bdcb9c1eed7a4f68539269dcf59.svg) the graph is compressed (or squished) towards the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis by a scale factor of ![](images/c7/c7432bf7a520e569e53fdd1a7303ce6b9f940d99e68b5e7ea3196c51acfe2b69.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/8d/8dc6f7e80ecaac9fa07890ce1881dd51de319597ae8b1dce821aa03c1f3eff06.svg) is between ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) and ![](images/76/76543f4805f22715457d55feb2a3c87d0d873bdcb9c1eed7a4f68539269dcf59.svg) the graph is stretched away from the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis by a scale factor of ![](images/c7/c7432bf7a520e569e53fdd1a7303ce6b9f940d99e68b5e7ea3196c51acfe2b69.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115420 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204085 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-66151cca-a80c-506c-9bd5-ec5398d6c5c9" "question-6fe4b67e-5906-58ff-aafc-e34173437530" "question-30241212-e00e-5866-b2f3-3d6f4d26064b" "question-5f065247-cbf1-5f49-a2d5-a7f84645d603"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203858 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z,w) = xyzw,$ compute $\\nabla_{x,w} \\: f.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\nabla_{x,w} \\: f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $w$ only.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyzw) & =yzw \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂w}=\\frac{∂}{∂w}(xyzw) & =xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂w}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}yzw \\\\ xyz\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204086 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-653a69c1-9d3e-5dbc-8e82-906415c5f4a6" "question-9a84fcca-bb7c-52d6-bb54-0e47f9a1105b" "question-c47abb70-6a87-5cce-aa76-b45ab7529754" "question-15f52165-8151-599f-bfe3-492fccf77d4a" "question-5cdbbb1b-f40a-59c6-9f85-65d64306e66f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203859 :question/problem "Given the function ![](images/df/dfff3e067517ee22189d8854be536b1c8955fb428ac7a7fc2297784243c058be.svg), compute ![](images/ce/ce5740fe7feadf98e660316bbe3d70877c382a03ae14748acfb749afa534aacf.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To compute ![](images/eb/eba21b079adea46f7772d028bf4d6371aa9afb7fa47bbc85b6c4a99a0bb2fe26.svg), we take partial derivatives with respect to ![](images/34/349015631ec44ef653936c85586d2c5ef56ba64392b3f92b4b92cbef3e731bc5.svg) and ![](images/a7/a77a353782459b753313d80235712695d65dc4a44d8d357aa41add6729f81d8f.svg).\n\n![](images/1b/1bfde37b8702b599011b80663b78ab27b17685480d50e9e8de578a92faccb880.svg)\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n![](images/15/15bd16ed0589950d568ccb7df71322f094a29760b735f668792668fd322027fb.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204087 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6a4dfa9a-b4b5-5892-ac39-cd310c33e22d" "question-8df7d96f-d828-5037-823d-2c01daf4d3bd" "question-eac2ec24-253b-5adc-871d-0bb7659e7619" "question-04bbc763-6f33-5643-bfea-ba99c7a847f8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203863 :question/problem "Given the function \n$f(\\mathbf{x}; \\mathbf{w}) = w_1x_1^{3} + w_2x_2^{3},$\ncompute $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}} \\: f$ with the input ![](images/35/352c09d0683235e0346b23eb4257530064bb733f3f75f3cfa0ae23cc7d9dde3e.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}} \\: f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $\\mathbf{w}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{3}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{3})={x}_{1}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{3}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{3})={x}_{2}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with ![](images/ee/ee17ff076572f222a01b38d6527cb22823a721fb5eb6afa33a65f93582b96589.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}27 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204088 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5ab6642a-36f8-590a-a631-0cf8a7d2875f" "question-b3c9497c-975d-55aa-bb92-1acbe4b3e0fa" "question-d8078445-7f57-54e9-9fc5-bfc3757a9189" "question-ef911a6f-8eaa-541f-ada4-10af4c9994c6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203860 :question/problem "Given the function $f(\\mathbf{x}; \\mathbf{w}) = (w_1x_1 - w_2+x_2)^2,$ compute $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}}(\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}} \\: f).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{w}} \\: f$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}+{x}_{2})^{2}=2{x}_{1}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}+{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}+{x}_{2})^{2}=-2({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}+{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}}=2{x}_{1}^{2}\\,\\, &  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}}=-2{x}_{1} \\\\  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}}=-2{x}_{1}\\,\\, &  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}({∇}_{w}\\,f) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2} & -2{x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-66151cca-a80c-506c-9bd5-ec5398d6c5c9" :question/id #uuid "66151cca-a80c-506c-9bd5-ec5398d6c5c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-264813" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{3}+{y}^{3}z,$ compute ${∇}_{x,z}\\,f.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{x,z}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{x,z}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $z$ only.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}+{y}^{3}z) & ={y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{3}+{y}^{3}z) & ={y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,z}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-42dafd0c-bde9-5b4c-a4ea-238f7c923d7b" "answer-field-f73ae8c8-8037-5e2b-8c0f-c094270e9fc9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-42dafd0c-bde9-5b4c-a4ea-238f7c923d7b" :answer-field/id #uuid "42dafd0c-bde9-5b4c-a4ea-238f7c923d7b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-687becec-31ee-5e27-af7c-cb59c4cf59fe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-687becec-31ee-5e27-af7c-cb59c4cf59fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-687becec-31ee-5e27-af7c-cb59c4cf59fe" :answer/id #uuid "687becec-31ee-5e27-af7c-cb59c4cf59fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y^3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f73ae8c8-8037-5e2b-8c0f-c094270e9fc9" :answer-field/id #uuid "f73ae8c8-8037-5e2b-8c0f-c094270e9fc9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-284471c3-f52f-5417-a7ef-94faf21efe3e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-284471c3-f52f-5417-a7ef-94faf21efe3e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-284471c3-f52f-5417-a7ef-94faf21efe3e" :answer/id #uuid "284471c3-f52f-5417-a7ef-94faf21efe3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y^3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6fe4b67e-5906-58ff-aafc-e34173437530" :question/id #uuid "6fe4b67e-5906-58ff-aafc-e34173437530" :question/math-academy-id "q-264817" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{xy}{z},$ compute ${∇}_{y,z}\\,f.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{y,z}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{y,z}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $y$ and $z$ only.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{xy}{z}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{xy}{z}) & =-\\frac{xy}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{y,z}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ -\\frac{xy}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2c1adc0a-c465-5765-a271-6cf456d2ee26" "answer-field-8a5606ec-22d8-59ef-accf-8e2b329fb9d3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2c1adc0a-c465-5765-a271-6cf456d2ee26" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1adc0a-c465-5765-a271-6cf456d2ee26" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c1b6661d-a08a-5c9c-97ea-75c8ed8b1a7e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c1b6661d-a08a-5c9c-97ea-75c8ed8b1a7e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1b6661d-a08a-5c9c-97ea-75c8ed8b1a7e" :answer/id #uuid "c1b6661d-a08a-5c9c-97ea-75c8ed8b1a7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{z}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8a5606ec-22d8-59ef-accf-8e2b329fb9d3" :answer-field/id #uuid "8a5606ec-22d8-59ef-accf-8e2b329fb9d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c49d497a-5133-5547-a1e5-47527e18b642" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c49d497a-5133-5547-a1e5-47527e18b642"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c49d497a-5133-5547-a1e5-47527e18b642" :answer/id #uuid "c49d497a-5133-5547-a1e5-47527e18b642" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-30241212-e00e-5866-b2f3-3d6f4d26064b" :question/id #uuid "30241212-e00e-5866-b2f3-3d6f4d26064b" :question/math-academy-id "q-264810" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{xyz},$ compute ${∇}_{x,y}\\,f.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{x,y}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{x,y}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $y$ only.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xyz}) & =yz{e}^{xyz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xyz}) & =xz{e}^{xyz}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,y}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}yz{e}^{xyz} \\\\ xz{e}^{xyz}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0871417-91fa-595c-885a-0195bb317353" "answer-field-afd5dbcb-74bc-5c07-a182-140a896ce903"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0871417-91fa-595c-885a-0195bb317353" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0871417-91fa-595c-885a-0195bb317353" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-db346e62-1def-5a72-9ff0-a38dc40f2ab5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db346e62-1def-5a72-9ff0-a38dc40f2ab5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db346e62-1def-5a72-9ff0-a38dc40f2ab5" :answer/id #uuid "db346e62-1def-5a72-9ff0-a38dc40f2ab5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "yz e^{xyz}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-afd5dbcb-74bc-5c07-a182-140a896ce903" :answer-field/id #uuid "afd5dbcb-74bc-5c07-a182-140a896ce903" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-78cd906e-dc0b-56ce-b0b6-24dfc3eeae85" :answer-field/choices ["answer-78cd906e-dc0b-56ce-b0b6-24dfc3eeae85"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78cd906e-dc0b-56ce-b0b6-24dfc3eeae85" :answer/id #uuid "78cd906e-dc0b-56ce-b0b6-24dfc3eeae85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xz e^{xyz}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204559 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+y{z}^{2},$ compute ${∇}_{x,y}\\,f.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{x,y}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{x,y}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $y$ only.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+y{z}^{2}) & =2xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+y{z}^{2}) & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,y}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy \\\\ {x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203321 :answer-field/correct "answer-120941c5-b24d-5fee-9a22-d751601b4a4f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-120941c5-b24d-5fee-9a22-d751601b4a4f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-120941c5-b24d-5fee-9a22-d751601b4a4f" :answer/id #uuid "120941c5-b24d-5fee-9a22-d751601b4a4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203322 :answer-field/correct "answer-c4c58225-5f24-5dd5-ab65-9e91f60c5343" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c4c58225-5f24-5dd5-ab65-9e91f60c5343"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4c58225-5f24-5dd5-ab65-9e91f60c5343" :answer/id #uuid "c4c58225-5f24-5dd5-ab65-9e91f60c5343" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5f065247-cbf1-5f49-a2d5-a7f84645d603" :question/id #uuid "5f065247-cbf1-5f49-a2d5-a7f84645d603" :question/math-academy-id "q-264821" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z,w)=zw{e}^{xy}$, compute ${∇}_{x,z}\\,f.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{x,z}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{x,z}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $z$ only.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(zw{e}^{xy}) & =yzw{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{∂}{∂z}(zw{e}^{xy}) & =w{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,z}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}yzw{e}^{xy} \\\\ w{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff841eb9-ea63-531b-90a6-805faeb34a87" "answer-field-ad5db1c2-7e76-5b04-be17-aecabe2de001"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff841eb9-ea63-531b-90a6-805faeb34a87" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff841eb9-ea63-531b-90a