"selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2e534c95-f518-53b6-a86e-97ebc796a9dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2e534c95-f518-53b6-a86e-97ebc796a9dc" "answer-00a47e43-e30e-5114-9ffb-a4bad244ed6a" "answer-fa799a0a-fb3a-50c2-8f68-0582a2824583" "answer-4dabe466-88e9-513a-a085-624722b4b785" "answer-33c3af05-d4bc-58d9-859f-9ed6f1f1fb54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e534c95-f518-53b6-a86e-97ebc796a9dc" :answer/id #uuid "2e534c95-f518-53b6-a86e-97ebc796a9dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a column vector of shape $3\\times 1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00a47e43-e30e-5114-9ffb-a4bad244ed6a" :answer/id #uuid "00a47e43-e30e-5114-9ffb-a4bad244ed6a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a matrix of shape $1\\times 1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa799a0a-fb3a-50c2-8f68-0582a2824583" :answer/id #uuid "fa799a0a-fb3a-50c2-8f68-0582a2824583" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a matrix of shape $3\\times 3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dabe466-88e9-513a-a085-624722b4b785" :answer/id #uuid "4dabe466-88e9-513a-a085-624722b4b785" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a row vector of shape $1\\times 3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33c3af05-d4bc-58d9-859f-9ed6f1f1fb54" :answer/id #uuid "33c3af05-d4bc-58d9-859f-9ed6f1f1fb54" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a scalar" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb5a1bbc-92e2-569a-8c18-7d0fed7e610f" :question/id #uuid "bb5a1bbc-92e2-569a-8c18-7d0fed7e610f" :question/math-academy-id "q-269512" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:R\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ {x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$" :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{3}$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ {x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2x \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{3\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d2ff3943-ac10-594c-b82e-a6425e8c3606"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d2ff3943-ac10-594c-b82e-a6425e8c3606" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ff3943-ac10-594c-b82e-a6425e8c3606" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-83b07263-56a1-53d6-9f6b-dd0ec4566cf5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c5326a22-d486-5ce3-9922-e1b26941c409" "answer-f5f8b85e-be47-53f6-a0d6-ac91b60f58ce" "answer-83b07263-56a1-53d6-9f6b-dd0ec4566cf5" "answer-b8433fa1-ff9b-5d58-8736-f6d088f0fbff" "answer-ae536f43-cb19-5d09-9654-9a4ddf9db7b3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5326a22-d486-5ce3-9922-e1b26941c409" :answer/id #uuid "c5326a22-d486-5ce3-9922-e1b26941c409" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & x & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5f8b85e-be47-53f6-a0d6-ac91b60f58ce" :answer/id #uuid "f5f8b85e-be47-53f6-a0d6-ac91b60f58ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2x & 3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83b07263-56a1-53d6-9f6b-dd0ec4566cf5" :answer/id #uuid "83b07263-56a1-53d6-9f6b-dd0ec4566cf5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2x \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8433fa1-ff9b-5d58-8736-f6d088f0fbff" :answer/id #uuid "b8433fa1-ff9b-5d58-8736-f6d088f0fbff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} \\\\ 3x\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae536f43-cb19-5d09-9654-9a4ddf9db7b3" :answer/id #uuid "ae536f43-cb19-5d09-9654-9a4ddf9db7b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ {x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-752cfa47-ef2c-5a18-9f32-7e84cd447c33" :question/id #uuid "752cfa47-ef2c-5a18-9f32-7e84cd447c33" :question/math-academy-id "q-269514" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:R\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{2}$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the total derivative at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9eafdd0c-fbfb-51de-93e2-baad15ae52e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9eafdd0c-fbfb-51de-93e2-baad15ae52e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "9eafdd0c-fbfb-51de-93e2-baad15ae52e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a025fb5b-6ee3-5ae3-a624-8c215ceb9820" :answer-field/choices ["answer-50f86596-75fe-59b7-9ec7-2bf9eedceca7" "answer-a025fb5b-6ee3-5ae3-a624-8c215ceb9820" "answer-12715d51-1e46-58dc-9ed7-12d504779850" "answer-62d3ff2d-74e4-54a7-bd2e-09536e6dc948" "answer-dae61b85-1333-5c21-bdf8-02a24c25d538"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50f86596-75fe-59b7-9ec7-2bf9eedceca7" :answer/id #uuid "50f86596-75fe-59b7-9ec7-2bf9eedceca7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a025fb5b-6ee3-5ae3-a624-8c215ceb9820" :answer/id #uuid "a025fb5b-6ee3-5ae3-a624-8c215ceb9820" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12715d51-1e46-58dc-9ed7-12d504779850" :answer/id #uuid "12715d51-1e46-58dc-9ed7-12d504779850" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62d3ff2d-74e4-54a7-bd2e-09536e6dc948" :answer/id #uuid "62d3ff2d-74e4-54a7-bd2e-09536e6dc948" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dae61b85-1333-5c21-bdf8-02a24c25d538" :answer/id #uuid "dae61b85-1333-5c21-bdf8-02a24c25d538" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-08a9562a-8441-5804-8199-2cecfe4f52dd" :question/id #uuid "08a9562a-8441-5804-8199-2cecfe4f52dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-269508" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.$ What is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a scalar-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3},$ and\n\n- outputs a scalar $f(x)\\in R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} & 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the total derivative at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}({x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0+3(2) & 1+2 & 3(1)+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd22ab92-c545-5049-be79-46a86bc87feb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd22ab92-c545-5049-be79-46a86bc87feb" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd22ab92-c545-5049-be79-46a86bc87feb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-04a78d66-ac2f-567f-a995-bb7404dc996f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4037b305-cfc6-556c-8651-d534e563bb62" "answer-16648459-62e9-5e7f-bf16-aea558ab3b77" "answer-5a590e93-aeaf-5485-9454-240d10475316" "answer-04a78d66-ac2f-567f-a995-bb7404dc996f" "answer-91f3e890-aa4e-5c31-9332-5127c036fff5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4037b305-cfc6-556c-8651-d534e563bb62" :answer/id #uuid "4037b305-cfc6-556c-8651-d534e563bb62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16648459-62e9-5e7f-bf16-aea558ab3b77" :answer/id #uuid "16648459-62e9-5e7f-bf16-aea558ab3b77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a590e93-aeaf-5485-9454-240d10475316" :answer/id #uuid "5a590e93-aeaf-5485-9454-240d10475316" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04a78d66-ac2f-567f-a995-bb7404dc996f" :answer/id #uuid "04a78d66-ac2f-567f-a995-bb7404dc996f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91f3e890-aa4e-5c31-9332-5127c036fff5" :answer/id #uuid "91f3e890-aa4e-5c31-9332-5127c036fff5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1b78008a-d228-5f88-9d88-9b7926941425" :question/id #uuid "1b78008a-d228-5f88-9d88-9b7926941425" :question/math-academy-id "q-269510" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot x,\n$$\n\nwhere the dot denotes matrix multiplication. What is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$" :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a scalar-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8b271751-4c56-517e-82e7-5413a260d685"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8b271751-4c56-517e-82e7-5413a260d685" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b271751-4c56-517e-82e7-5413a260d685" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6d60435b-ec88-5c57-8c2f-2268a47398b5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f034c121-3677-5359-8feb-3f7fcbaa492b" "answer-6d60435b-ec88-5c57-8c2f-2268a47398b5" "answer-de8edb05-08f9-5f50-905f-b752f7df4d7f" "answer-36eda5b5-aaa3-50f4-808e-2ebbafdb8d01" "answer-eb57ae0c-4948-524a-bf84-e3e8209d04b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f034c121-3677-5359-8feb-3f7fcbaa492b" :answer/id #uuid "f034c121-3677-5359-8feb-3f7fcbaa492b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d60435b-ec88-5c57-8c2f-2268a47398b5" :answer/id #uuid "6d60435b-ec88-5c57-8c2f-2268a47398b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de8edb05-08f9-5f50-905f-b752f7df4d7f" :answer/id #uuid "de8edb05-08f9-5f50-905f-b752f7df4d7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36eda5b5-aaa3-50f4-808e-2ebbafdb8d01" :answer/id #uuid "36eda5b5-aaa3-50f4-808e-2ebbafdb8d01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} & 2{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb57ae0c-4948-524a-bf84-e3e8209d04b6" :answer/id #uuid "eb57ae0c-4948-524a-bf84-e3e8209d04b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}{x}_{2} & 6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204580 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:R\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$" :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{2}$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203413 :answer-field/correct "answer-8f3d1b6a-19cd-52a6-8624-ea921d4995d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60be3a32-9634-5fc9-af68-57501017072c" "answer-83803cc0-4654-56d7-9011-c919d719793e" "answer-312c6c6d-408c-53ac-981e-a9f933aff470" "answer-946e9d66-f055-53a6-a7ab-d00d690fa74a" "answer-8f3d1b6a-19cd-52a6-8624-ea921d4995d1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60be3a32-9634-5fc9-af68-57501017072c" :answer/id #uuid "60be3a32-9634-5fc9-af68-57501017072c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83803cc0-4654-56d7-9011-c919d719793e" :answer/id #uuid "83803cc0-4654-56d7-9011-c919d719793e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-312c6c6d-408c-53ac-981e-a9f933aff470" :answer/id #uuid "312c6c6d-408c-53ac-981e-a9f933aff470" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-946e9d66-f055-53a6-a7ab-d00d690fa74a" :answer/id #uuid "946e9d66-f055-53a6-a7ab-d00d690fa74a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2x & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f3d1b6a-19cd-52a6-8624-ea921d4995d1" :answer/id #uuid "8f3d1b6a-19cd-52a6-8624-ea921d4995d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2x \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e0e9fc0e-4ca3-5c69-92b6-288fedf51d5a" :question/id #uuid "e0e9fc0e-4ca3-5c69-92b6-288fedf51d5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-269665" :question/problem "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to {R}^{2}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]A.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{a}_{22}}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\nf(A)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{11}-2{a}_{21}+3{a}_{31} & 2{a}_{12}-2{a}_{22}+3{a}_{32}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{a}_{22}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{22}}(2{a}_{12}-2{a}_{22}+3{a}_{32})=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7fe390c4-afd7-57df-9245-b9ef90284a05"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7fe390c4-afd7-57df-9245-b9ef90284a05" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe390c4-afd7-57df-9245-b9ef90284a05" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-96a26531-d38a-52e9-b3c9-24c8415ea4df" :answer-field/choices ["answer-96a26531-d38a-52e9-b3c9-24c8415ea4df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96a26531-d38a-52e9-b3c9-24c8415ea4df" :answer/id #uuid "96a26531-d38a-52e9-b3c9-24c8415ea4df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204575 :question/problem "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=A[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{32}}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\nf(A)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}+2{a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21}+2{a}_{22} \\\\ {a}_{31}+2{a}_{32}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{32}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{32}}({a}_{31}+2{a}_{32})=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203388 :answer-field/correct "answer-b4cfa01d-2d08-5b77-b97b-525e3855efcf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b4cfa01d-2d08-5b77-b97b-525e3855efcf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4cfa01d-2d08-5b77-b97b-525e3855efcf" :answer/id #uuid "b4cfa01d-2d08-5b77-b97b-525e3855efcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a0562d85-9065-5ae6-9356-9eba869acb60" :question/id #uuid "a0562d85-9065-5ae6-9356-9eba869acb60" :question/math-academy-id "q-269667" :question/problem "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to {R}^{2}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]A.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{a}_{32}}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\nf(A)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{11}+{a}_{21}-2{a}_{31} & 5{a}_{12}+{a}_{22}-2{a}_{32}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{a}_{32}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{32}}(5{a}_{11}+{a}_{21}-2{a}_{31})=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7978731e-1bfd-5eec-8332-24f5f00d8343"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7978731e-1bfd-5eec-8332-24f5f00d8343" :answer-field/id #uuid "7978731e-1bfd-5eec-8332-24f5f00d8343" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-926d713a-df65-5783-bd0c-dd44262809ae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-926d713a-df65-5783-bd0c-dd44262809ae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-926d713a-df65-5783-bd0c-dd44262809ae" :answer/id #uuid "926d713a-df65-5783-bd0c-dd44262809ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-085bdd72-4fe3-54d7-bc86-1b96c914a54e" :question/id #uuid "085bdd72-4fe3-54d7-bc86-1b96c914a54e" :question/math-academy-id "q-269655" :question/problem "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 3}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=A[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{31}}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\nf(A)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{11}+{a}_{12}+4{a}_{13} \\\\ 5{a}_{21}+{a}_{22}+4{a}_{23} \\\\ 5{a}_{31}+{a}_{32}+4{a}_{33}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{31}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{31}}(5{a}_{31}+{a}_{32}+4{a}_{33})=5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-65083882-b071-5cdc-b0bb-b6b399d89d5d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-65083882-b071-5cdc-b0bb-b6b399d89d5d" :answer-field/id #uuid "65083882-b071-5cdc-b0bb-b6b399d89d5d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8a76f9d5-8bc8-5cdd-9750-c5367f8d4ee6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8a76f9d5-8bc8-5cdd-9750-c5367f8d4ee6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a76f9d5-8bc8-5cdd-9750-c5367f8d4ee6" :answer/id #uuid "8a76f9d5-8bc8-5cdd-9750-c5367f8d4ee6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2dd288c1-a4fd-5154-a46e-c65943d99445" :question/id #uuid "2dd288c1-a4fd-5154-a46e-c65943d99445" :question/math-academy-id "q-269669" :question/problem "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 3}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -6 & -5\\end{aligned}]A.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{33}}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -6 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10{a}_{11}-6{a}_{21}-5{a}_{31}\\, & 10{a}_{12}-6{a}_{22}-5{a}_{32}\\, & 10{a}_{13}-6{a}_{23}-5{a}_{33}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{33}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{33}}(10{a}_{13}-6{a}_{23}-5{a}_{33})=-5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e16ff3f-f740-5551-904f-07d98a46fde3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e16ff3f-f740-5551-904f-07d98a46fde3" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e16ff3f-f740-5551-904f-07d98a46fde3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6d6bd45e-d24b-549d-9a1c-62a7a41a0df6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6d6bd45e-d24b-549d-9a1c-62a7a41a0df6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d6bd45e-d24b-549d-9a1c-62a7a41a0df6" :answer/id #uuid "6d6bd45e-d24b-549d-9a1c-62a7a41a0df6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205188 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we’ll continue exploring the *total derivative* of a vector-valued function, which generalizes the derivative to functions with multiple inputs and outputs.\n\nSuppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/cd/cd9132d6a0e934349ae58a0718e6320cba3dd8ae84de7df2e3fb98b391256b57.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables.\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the **total derivative** of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/c8/c8fbc309a129b8e19db46aebff637e47259b629ef83c282a38b3b318c3a4f269.svg)\n\nThis is sometimes called the **Jacobian matrix** of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x}$. Each row corresponds to the derivative of one component function $f_i$, and each column corresponds to the rate of change of all the components of $\\boldsymbol f$ with respect to one input variable $x_j$. This is sometimes called the **numerator layout** for derivatives because each row corresponds to each function component, which appears in the numerator positions of the partial derivatives.\n\nNotice that if $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a scalar-valued function, then the total derivative is a row vector given by\n  ![](images/6e/6ef756e82eb71d528a8f279863fd86efc8669658456a65879fba73d08e964bd5.svg)\n\nOn the other hand, if $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ is a vector-valued function of a single variable, then the total derivative is a column vector\n  ![](images/cd/cd232968d5ffff3040294f8093a42c91bccd3207f91bfc2f784cb53cad00441d.svg)\n\nNow let’s get some practice by working through a few examples of total derivatives." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205193 :tutorial/content "Let’s examine examples of functions defined by a matrix of constants multiplied by a vector more carefully.\n\nConsider the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^{2} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ defined by\n![](images/f8/f81cbd9b797d1f1b05edd5f254d6735456e937355fe0e6692a4a830da925351b.svg) \n\nSuppose we want to compute the total derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}$ at the point ![](images/cd/cd0b094634acde36ee93c2fb90c6f11ab56c2f675d27ba65077542d75cb451d6.svg).\n\nThe total derivative of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^{n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^m$ with respect to a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is the $m \\times n$ matrix\n![](images/63/6305cfe857f2228d471b85f859302a43046470f1d182b34abbf3ab4b362e7e07.svg)\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ where ![](images/a8/a8d36d7195af007d90a4f62cf213e28c7f51ced97168fb16d31a2c9192799b53.svg), we get\n![](images/79/79110c30e4f827ccc401c0d8d9b8a08f35a88d12781d83c7e365171de0bbd67d.svg)\n\nFrom here, we have \n\n$$\nf_1(\\mathbf{x}) = 3x_1 + 0 \\cdot x_2, \\qquad f_2(\\mathbf{x}) = x_1 - x_2,\\qquad f_3(\\mathbf{x}) = 0 \\cdot x_1 + 2x_2.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is independent of $\\mathbf x,$ we also have\n\n![](images/c1/c187256b1ad2181f53482829d4d5076b0b09fc19f1d399aa41fb9da6ca0bc8f3.svg)\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}$ is constant and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n,$ then\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = A}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205189 :tutorial/content "We've learned that the derivative of a vector-valued function is a Jacobian matrix. Now imagine the input to our function $\\mathbf f$ is a matrix instead of a vector. In such cases, the Jacobian matrix must expand to account for two input dimensions.\n\nSuppose we have a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\to \\mathbb{R}^p$ that maps a matrix $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ to a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{A}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$, defined as\n\n![](images/f7/f7b9ca5880f164c28b490fd4a215a56769e426bdde8111a24e6530c03d83b937.svg)\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a scalar-valued function of all the entries of the matrix $\\mathbf{A}.$\n\nSince the input $\\mathbf{A}$ varies along two dimensions (rows and columns), and the output $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{A})$ varies along one (a vector with $p$ components), the total derivative of $\\mathbf f,$ which we denote as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{A}}\n$$\n\nis naturally represented as a *three-dimensional object!* The technical name for this object is a **tensor**. Since it is a three-dimensional object, this particular tensor is a **third-order tensor** (or $3$-**tensor**).\n\nTo capture a meaningful derivative, we must differentiate each component $f_i$ with respect to *every element of the matrix* $\\mathbf A.$ We can visualize this as a stack of matrices, or layers, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d533d4d2e387c8c9f177b9dafe939b58c1f1d7db2f03e9df2059fa9f7cbe22c5.png)\n\nLet's apply this concept to a specific example in the next slide." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205216 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the function $\\mathbf f: \\mathbb R^{3\\times2} \\to \\mathbb R^{2},$ defined as\n\n![](images/8d/8d7f629f4b82087a1d23ef1253ce7eb98ca760310d92a2ac8cf8c3fd3a4d9473.svg)\n\nWe calculate the derivatives of $\\mathbf f$ layer by layer as follows:\n\n- **Layer 1**: The derivatives of the first component $f_1$ with respect to $\\mathbf A.$\n![](images/c4/c4dab9446396e82d20ebbb38e3aabce9908565aeb7aeb48f2804a271d6ddf66f.svg)\n\n- **Layer 2**: The derivatives of the second component $f_2$ with respect to $\\mathbf A.$\n![](images/ce/ce7e31c8c3faf75aa7980a0e4f5f707f84f116e97b30c8b0dbd504bcfb4822c8.svg)\n\nCombining these layers into a 3D block, we get the total derivative (a $3$-tensor in $\\mathbb R^{2\\times3\\times2}$):\n\n![](images/3d/3d6eeebb92218d4f0995dbccd74b444da112f0e55b22ade0bbaa0259e06e9bb0.png)\n\nYou've probably realized by now that working with 3-tensors directly is cumbersome! So, rather than trying to visualize the full tensor all at once, focusing on its components is often helpful. Generally,\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{A}} \\right)_{i, j, k} = \\dfrac{\\partial f_i}{\\partial a_{jk}}\n$$\n\nis a *scalar* representing the derivative of the $i$th output component with respect to the element $a_{jk}$ of the matrix $\\mathbf A.$\n\nFor example, the derivative of $f_{\\color{red}1}$ with respect to $a_{{\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}2}}$ corresponds to the component at index $({\\color{red}1},{\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}2})$ of a tensor. In our example, we saw that\n![](images/4f/4fa3d21f5d62bd01491c49b3351cdd3cf751a7ad793509d1c2b3846e4f6825cd.svg)\n\nLooking at the ${\\color{red}1}$st layer (front face), row ${\\color{blue}3},$ column ${\\color{blue}2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf A}\\right)_{{\\color{red}1}, {\\color{blue}3}, {\\color{blue}2}} = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}.\n$$\n\nDerivatives of matrix functions arise in optimization problems, machine learning (e.g., backpropagation through layers), and physics (e.g., tensor fields). Understanding their structure is key to efficient computation and interpretation." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204180 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186174436 :answer-field/choices 17592186174437] [:db/retract 17592186174436 :answer-field/choices 17592186174438] [:db/retract 17592186174436 :answer-field/choices 17592186174439] [:db/retract 17592186174436 :answer-field/choices 17592186174440] [:db/retract 17592186174436 :answer-field/choices 17592186174441] {:db/id 17592186175948 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-71001133-6021-56cc-9d7c-8178573baa4b" "question-9b8b4204-5ca8-58b7-aeeb-092f50cd6610" "question-48d3004f-21c4-5fdb-bf3a-f534994324c2" "question-7ccf1362-83a8-5f48-9ecd-741f9bd50f82"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5ce7ead8-e0e1-5d3e-bcf5-334f7b5e90bb" "question-7f3e3995-bd16-5e9f-8391-9d6b04505974"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175802 :question/problem "Given that the power series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n (x-2)^n$ converges at  $x=6,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nI. The series converges at $x=0$\n\nII. The series converges at $x=7$\n\nIII. The series converges at $x=-2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-71001133-6021-56cc-9d7c-8178573baa4b" :question/id #uuid "71001133-6021-56cc-9d7c-8178573baa4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-354475" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{n}^{3}(5x+10)^{n}=(5x+10)+{2}^{3}(5x+10)^{2}+{3}^{3}(5x+10)^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{3}(5x+10)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(5x+10)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}(5x+10)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(5x+10)}{{n}^{3}}| \\\\  & =|5x+10|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{3} \\\\  & =|5x+10|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5|x+2|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x+2|<\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a603830a-8c20-51b7-9e0a-19f2394ee249"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a603830a-8c20-51b7-9e0a-19f2394ee249" :answer-field/id #uuid "a603830a-8c20-51b7-9e0a-19f2394ee249" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-21926209-1e77-5b82-bb46-f475fa5747ee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-21926209-1e77-5b82-bb46-f475fa5747ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21926209-1e77-5b82-bb46-f475fa5747ee" :answer/id #uuid "21926209-1e77-5b82-bb46-f475fa5747ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9b8b4204-5ca8-58b7-aeeb-092f50cd6610" :question/id #uuid "9b8b4204-5ca8-58b7-aeeb-092f50cd6610" :question/math-academy-id "q-351686" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(3x-6)^{n}}{(n!)^{2}}=(3x-6)+\\frac{(3x-6)^{2}}{(2!)^{2}}+\\frac{(3x-6)^{3}}{(3!)^{2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(3x-6)^{n}}{(n!)^{2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(3x-6)^{n+1}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}}{\\frac{(3x-6)^{n}}{(n!)^{2}}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(3x-6)^{n+1}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n!)^{2}}{(3x-6)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|3x-6|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =|3x-6|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d8e9b55-92ac-52a8-aa96-387c3f2de926"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d8e9b55-92ac-52a8-aa96-387c3f2de926" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d8e9b55-92ac-52a8-aa96-387c3f2de926" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fce48f88-30e2-54a5-a510-d7f122a28115" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fce48f88-30e2-54a5-a510-d7f122a28115"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fce48f88-30e2-54a5-a510-d7f122a28115" :answer/id #uuid "fce48f88-30e2-54a5-a510-d7f122a28115" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-48d3004f-21c4-5fdb-bf3a-f534994324c2" :question/id #uuid "48d3004f-21c4-5fdb-bf3a-f534994324c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-351678" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{n}^{4}(3x-3)^{n}=(3x-3)+{2}^{4}(3x-3)^{2}+{3}^{4}(3x-3)^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{4}(3x-3)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{4}(3x-3)^{n+1}}{{n}^{4}(3x-3)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{4}(3x-3)}{{n}^{4}}| \\\\  & =|3x-3|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{4} \\\\  & =|3x-3|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x-1|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-1|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f9008fa5-bcba-5a22-b50b-b30fa83037e9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f9008fa5-bcba-5a22-b50b-b30fa83037e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "f9008fa5-bcba-5a22-b50b-b30fa83037e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2aa10fa5-bc74-53cb-82c6-c292399809ba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2aa10fa5-bc74-53cb-82c6-c292399809ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2aa10fa5-bc74-53cb-82c6-c292399809ba" :answer/id #uuid "2aa10fa5-bc74-53cb-82c6-c292399809ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7ccf1362-83a8-5f48-9ecd-741f9bd50f82" :question/id #uuid "7ccf1362-83a8-5f48-9ecd-741f9bd50f82" :question/math-academy-id "q-351685" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}{n!}=(x+1)+\\frac{{2}^{2}(x+1)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{3}^{2}(x+1)^{3}}{3!}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(x+1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}{\\frac{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}{n!}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(x+1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|x+1|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n+1}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =|x+1|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5794ddb-77c8-5f58-be53-19c769b0ae50"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5794ddb-77c8-5f58-be53-19c769b0ae50" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5794ddb-77c8-5f58-be53-19c769b0ae50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2b037f89-6e34-5cac-bf49-6949b804db17" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b037f89-6e34-5cac-bf49-6949b804db17"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b037f89-6e34-5cac-bf49-6949b804db17" :answer/id #uuid "2b037f89-6e34-5cac-bf49-6949b804db17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176363 :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{2(n+1)-1}}{2(n+1)-1}\\cdot \\frac{2n-1}{(x-1)^{2n-1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{2n+1}}{2n+1}\\cdot \\frac{2n-1}{(x-1)^{2n-1}}| \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2n-1}{2n+1}) \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{(2-\\frac{1}{n})}{(2+\\frac{1}{n})}) \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-1{|}^{2} & <1 \\\\ |x-1| & <1 \\\\ -1<x-1 & <1 \\\\ 0<x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(0,2).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x-1|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{((0)-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}-⋯ \\\\  & =-(1+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{5}+⋯)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series in parentheses with ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n},$ we see that the series is divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- When $x=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{((2)-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{5}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series with ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n},$ we see that the series is divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\nSo the interval of convergence is $I=(0,2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174436 :answer-field/correct "answer-ac66bd7b-5f2c-5f83-b234-a44f9c1dabb6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac66bd7b-5f2c-5f83-b234-a44f9c1dabb6" "answer-c505fa40-bc71-5bac-b182-da34ecdbd863" "answer-60509868-fe58-5055-98d4-86f8dd815071" "answer-fd5f0831-9451-54cb-98ea-9585f500774d" "answer-248f0c77-27a3-5962-98e6-5d14c1f94958"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac66bd7b-5f2c-5f83-b234-a44f9c1dabb6" :answer/id #uuid "ac66bd7b-5f2c-5f83-b234-a44f9c1dabb6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=(0,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c505fa40-bc71-5bac-b182-da34ecdbd863" :answer/id #uuid "c505fa40-bc71-5bac-b182-da34ecdbd863" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=(0,2]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60509868-fe58-5055-98d4-86f8dd815071" :answer/id #uuid "60509868-fe58-5055-98d4-86f8dd815071" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[0,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd5f0831-9451-54cb-98ea-9585f500774d" :answer/id #uuid "fd5f0831-9451-54cb-98ea-9585f500774d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[0,2]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-248f0c77-27a3-5962-98e6-5d14c1f94958" :answer/id #uuid "248f0c77-27a3-5962-98e6-5d14c1f94958" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R=\\infty $ and $I=(-\\infty ,\\infty )$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5ce7ead8-e0e1-5d3e-bcf5-334f7b5e90bb" :question/id #uuid "5ce7ead8-e0e1-5d3e-bcf5-334f7b5e90bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103164" :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-6)^{n}$ converges at  $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nI. The series converges at $x=2$\n\nII. The series converges at $x=4$\n\nIII. The series diverges at $x=\\frac{7}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=6,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $\\frac{7}{2},$ i.e., $R\\ge \\frac{7}{2}.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}).$\n\n![](images/f7/f7b2a20d097d3ff34f92ff7f0015c449150575420dd53132d0b2703567628ef5.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $2\\notin [\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $4\\in [\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}),$ the series is convergent at $x=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\frac{7}{2}\\in [\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}),$ the series is convergent at $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d9087408-87a7-54e8-a968-c924e6aef9fc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d9087408-87a7-54e8-a968-c924e6aef9fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "d9087408-87a7-54e8-a968-c924e6aef9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1319c2ec-618b-5116-84ad-c35aea773f6c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b13d80e3-df35-5ed6-bfb9-d837654b920e" "answer-9bffd418-6b37-587c-8cde-087815d0ad24" "answer-1319c2ec-618b-5116-84ad-c35aea773f6c" "answer-6e81d8e3-ba9c-53f4-b41b-54bcd26d2317" "answer-0482be27-164a-5592-8483-f323a3a40616"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b13d80e3-df35-5ed6-bfb9-d837654b920e" :answer/id #uuid "b13d80e3-df35-5ed6-bfb9-d837654b920e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bffd418-6b37-587c-8cde-087815d0ad24" :answer/id #uuid "9bffd418-6b37-587c-8cde-087815d0ad24" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1319c2ec-618b-5116-84ad-c35aea773f6c" :answer/id #uuid "1319c2ec-618b-5116-84ad-c35aea773f6c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e81d8e3-ba9c-53f4-b41b-54bcd26d2317" :answer/id #uuid "6e81d8e3-ba9c-53f4-b41b-54bcd26d2317" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0482be27-164a-5592-8483-f323a3a40616" :answer/id #uuid "0482be27-164a-5592-8483-f323a3a40616" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7f3e3995-bd16-5e9f-8391-9d6b04505974" :question/id #uuid "7f3e3995-bd16-5e9f-8391-9d6b04505974" :question/math-academy-id "q-103153" :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-4)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nI. The series diverges at $x=3$\n\nII. The series converges at $x=-2$\n\nIII. The series converges at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=4,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $5,$ i.e., $R\\ge 5.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-1,9).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-1,9).$\n\n![](images/c0/c0f5ebe0de173431758aed0680a6795267582aa2bc209702253d3d2828c91d02.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $3\\in [-1,9),$ the series is convergent at $x=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $-2\\notin [-1,9),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $1\\in [-1,9),$ the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f93a7063-e752-50fc-bfa7-29ed5bfc2348"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f93a7063-e752-50fc-bfa7-29ed5bfc2348" :answer-field/id #uuid "f93a7063-e752-50fc-bfa7-29ed5bfc2348" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fefdd098-4a05-5d8b-867b-c67a1ae4e9d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-92a7d378-3b14-57c4-9c58-a2e3ff3b2b37" "answer-89319f45-9bfa-5816-89b8-0070ff4bd252" "answer-215f9b3a-91a9-566c-b8af-8ec3793e8073" "answer-2facdcb7-e6af-58cf-92f3-c61ce6fddc16" "answer-fefdd098-4a05-5d8b-867b-c67a1ae4e9d1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92a7d378-3b14-57c4-9c58-a2e3ff3b2b37" :answer/id #uuid "92a7d378-3b14-57c4-9c58-a2e3ff3b2b37" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89319f45-9bfa-5816-89b8-0070ff4bd252" :answer/id #uuid "89319f45-9bfa-5816-89b8-0070ff4bd252" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-215f9b3a-91a9-566c-b8af-8ec3793e8073" :answer/id #uuid "215f9b3a-91a9-566c-b8af-8ec3793e8073" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2facdcb7-e6af-58cf-92f3-c61ce6fddc16" :answer/id #uuid "2facdcb7-e6af-58cf-92f3-c61ce6fddc16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fefdd098-4a05-5d8b-867b-c67a1ae4e9d1" :answer/id #uuid "fefdd098-4a05-5d8b-867b-c67a1ae4e9d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176111 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186397210 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-14\\lambda {y}^{′}+49{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e08589a3-02d8-594e-8593-48db9790c753" :activity/id #uuid "e08589a3-02d8-594e-8593-48db9790c753" :activity/title "Eigenvalues and Eigenfunctions of Homogeneous BVPs" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049830 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-216f8e14-7c94-5ef7-babd-78281fa1fe2d" "step-f13d3f5c-4669-55f0-8d8e-50bb1b38e0c6" "step-657eea8d-526f-550d-b00d-4e37eca243c7" "step-636715cd-0d7e-58bd-89a4-fedf8f61d582" "step-d86b4168-283b-56b1-8e35-bb1e41b82281"] :activity/first-step "step-216f8e14-7c94-5ef7-babd-78281fa1fe2d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-216f8e14-7c94-5ef7-babd-78281fa1fe2d" :step/id #uuid "216f8e14-7c94-5ef7-babd-78281fa1fe2d" :step/content 17592186167269 :step/next "step-f13d3f5c-4669-55f0-8d8e-50bb1b38e0c6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f13d3f5c-4669-55f0-8d8e-50bb1b38e0c6" :step/id #uuid "f13d3f5c-4669-55f0-8d8e-50bb1b38e0c6" :step/content 17592186397210 :step/next "step-657eea8d-526f-550d-b00d-4e37eca243c7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-657eea8d-526f-550d-b00d-4e37eca243c7" :step/id #uuid "657eea8d-526f-550d-b00d-4e37eca243c7" :step/content 17592186397252 :step/next "step-636715cd-0d7e-58bd-89a4-fedf8f61d582" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-636715cd-0d7e-58bd-89a4-fedf8f61d582" :step/id #uuid "636715cd-0d7e-58bd-89a4-fedf8f61d582" :step/content 17592186391695 :step/next "step-d86b4168-283b-56b1-8e35-bb1e41b82281" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d86b4168-283b-56b1-8e35-bb1e41b82281" :step/id #uuid "d86b4168-283b-56b1-8e35-bb1e41b82281" :step/content 17592186314746 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068765 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b1273579-55cb-579f-b627-8d3491d58407" "question-6e107289-3e5f-5156-a2e0-991fb3d001dd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068511 :question/problem "Solve for $n$ where $n^2 +12n = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+12n & =0 \\\\ n(n+12) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get \n\n$$\nn=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad n+12=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives \n\n$$\nn=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad n=-12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $n=-12,0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068766 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-51c36057-7763-517f-b083-7d2e76df4e9d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068512 :question/problem "Solve $3p^2 = 5p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t3p^2 &= 5p \\[3pt]\n\t3p^2 - 5p &= 0 \\[3pt]\n\tp(3p - 5) &= 0\n}\n$$\n\nSo, by the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\np=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 3p-5=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives \n\n$$\np=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad p=\\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p=0,\\dfrac{5}{3}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068767 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bfb7af78-939c-52a0-a55c-b15bc79862e1" "question-a8446f82-4129-51d2-b187-039c54ac0008"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068513 :question/problem "Solve the equation  $6x^2 +2x -2= 8x^2- x -2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}+2x-2 & =8{x}^{2}-x-2 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+2x-2-(8{x}^{2}-x-2) & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+2x-2-8{x}^{2}+x+2 & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{2}-8{x}^{2})+(2x+x)+(-2+2) & =0 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}+3x+0 & =0 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ x(-2x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad -2x + 3=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives \n\n$$\nx=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=0,\\dfrac{3}{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b1273579-55cb-579f-b627-8d3491d58407" :question/id #uuid "b1273579-55cb-579f-b627-8d3491d58407" :question/math-academy-id "q-223708" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the solutions to ${y}^{2}-y=0$ are $y=${{field-1}} and $y=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ y(y-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\ny=0\\,\\text{or}\\,y-1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\ny=0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=0$ and $y=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-13097025-abc7-5fd8-ac39-c0822ec5c38a" "answer-field-3c399da4-ca9e-5974-bb9e-d6c133075b6b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-13097025-abc7-5fd8-ac39-c0822ec5c38a" :answer-field/id #uuid "13097025-abc7-5fd8-ac39-c0822ec5c38a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-063b4024-4346-54ee-8c39-f524da9d179e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-063b4024-4346-54ee-8c39-f524da9d179e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-063b4024-4346-54ee-8c39-f524da9d179e" :answer/id #uuid "063b4024-4346-54ee-8c39-f524da9d179e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3c399da4-ca9e-5974-bb9e-d6c133075b6b" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c399da4-ca9e-5974-bb9e-d6c133075b6b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-731fcee1-1015-58b3-a75b-a6066cd86086" :answer-field/choices ["answer-731fcee1-1015-58b3-a75b-a6066cd86086"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-731fcee1-1015-58b3-a75b-a6066cd86086" :answer/id #uuid "731fcee1-1015-58b3-a75b-a6066cd86086" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6e107289-3e5f-5156-a2e0-991fb3d001dd" :question/id #uuid "6e107289-3e5f-5156-a2e0-991fb3d001dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-223721" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the solutions to $4{m}^{2}+5m=0$ are $m=${{field-1}} and $m=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{m}^{2}+5m & =0 \\\\ m(4m+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\nm=0\\,\\text{or}\\,4m+5=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nm=0\\,\\text{or}\\,m=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $m=-\\frac{5}{4}$ and $m=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1ec19aa7-3843-53b4-ad93-0c17da1d1f0a" "answer-field-f44ed7f0-c74c-51b2-b88d-368b62c92945"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1ec19aa7-3843-53b4-ad93-0c17da1d1f0a" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec19aa7-3843-53b4-ad93-0c17da1d1f0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-629e6591-71d0-53ae-a34c-1497f4a3b29c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-629e6591-71d0-53ae-a34c-1497f4a3b29c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-629e6591-71d0-53ae-a34c-1497f4a3b29c" :answer/id #uuid "629e6591-71d0-53ae-a34c-1497f4a3b29c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f44ed7f0-c74c-51b2-b88d-368b62c92945" :answer-field/id #uuid "f44ed7f0-c74c-51b2-b88d-368b62c92945" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cd20007e-a1bf-5680-a2d9-48f921d0d14c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cd20007e-a1bf-5680-a2d9-48f921d0d14c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd20007e-a1bf-5680-a2d9-48f921d0d14c" :answer/id #uuid "cd20007e-a1bf-5680-a2d9-48f921d0d14c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069161 :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the solutions to $\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}=-2z$ are $z=${{field-1}} and $z=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2} & =-2z \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}+2z & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}z(z+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}z=0\\,\\text{or}\\,z+5=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nz=0\\,\\text{or}\\,z=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=-5$ and $z=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066208 :answer-field/correct "answer-f6541888-fe03-58d9-8138-dadc1e8f6450" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f6541888-fe03-58d9-8138-dadc1e8f6450"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6541888-fe03-58d9-8138-dadc1e8f6450" :answer/id #uuid "f6541888-fe03-58d9-8138-dadc1e8f6450" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066207 :answer-field/correct "answer-08260f9d-0f7d-5e3a-875c-8660c7a3982f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-08260f9d-0f7d-5e3a-875c-8660c7a3982f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08260f9d-0f7d-5e3a-875c-8660c7a3982f" :answer/id #uuid "08260f9d-0f7d-5e3a-875c-8660c7a3982f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-51c36057-7763-517f-b083-7d2e76df4e9d" :question/id #uuid "51c36057-7763-517f-b083-7d2e76df4e9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-220342" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the solutions to $3{t}^{2}=\\frac{t}{3}$ are $t=${{field-1}} and $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} & =\\frac{t}{3} \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{t}{3} & =0 \\\\ t(3t-\\frac{1}{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\nt=0\\,\\text{or}\\,3t-\\frac{1}{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nt=0\\,\\text{or}\\,t=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{1}{9}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-81ff2d58-8983-5b2d-9543-a2ae613d6fa6" "answer-field-202e3786-76c0-5805-95d7-8f7c83dc23c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-81ff2d58-8983-5b2d-9543-a2ae613d6fa6" :answer-field/id #uuid "81ff2d58-8983-5b2d-9543-a2ae613d6fa6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7bd5f524-9ed8-5a26-bac4-febe5ddeb203" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7bd5f524-9ed8-5a26-bac4-febe5ddeb203"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bd5f524-9ed8-5a26-bac4-febe5ddeb203" :answer/id #uuid "7bd5f524-9ed8-5a26-bac4-febe5ddeb203" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-202e3786-76c0-5805-95d7-8f7c83dc23c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "202e3786-76c0-5805-95d7-8f7c83dc23c3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2d871265-9745-50ac-9f9f-ff7a82bad2fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2d871265-9745-50ac-9f9f-ff7a82bad2fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d871265-9745-50ac-9f9f-ff7a82bad2fc" :answer/id #uuid "2d871265-9745-50ac-9f9f-ff7a82bad2fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bfb7af78-939c-52a0-a55c-b15bc79862e1" :question/id #uuid "bfb7af78-939c-52a0-a55c-b15bc79862e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-23715" :question/problem "Solve the equation $4{z}^{2}+7z-1=3{z}^{2}+3z-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}+7z-1 & =3{z}^{2}+3z-1 \\\\ (4{z}^{2}+7z-1)-(3{z}^{2}+3z-1) & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+7z-1-3{z}^{2}-3z+1 & =0 \\\\ (4{z}^{2}-3{z}^{2})+(7z-3z)+(-1+1) & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}+4z & =0 \\\\ z(z+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\nz=0\\,\\text{or}\\,z+4=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nz=0\\,\\text{or}\\,z=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=0,-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e8df31f3-59b3-58f6-8b3d-b72e6e014b3c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8df31f3-59b3-58f6-8b3d-b72e6e014b3c" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8df31f3-59b3-58f6-8b3d-b72e6e014b3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ec0072e3-ac11-5027-8672-d8f98ce2d6a6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f41bf8f6-3517-583a-8ca4-fdcd7e69df4b" "answer-b16ea246-d4c6-54c5-a144-ce73e8205a6c" "answer-ec0072e3-ac11-5027-8672-d8f98ce2d6a6" "answer-a26bf7df-34a2-59ce-a047-995bb3f92dd7" "answer-ce81a382-d2c4-543c-9aa2-f3d3ef87a4ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f41bf8f6-3517-583a-8ca4-fdcd7e69df4b" :answer/id #uuid "f41bf8f6-3517-583a-8ca4-fdcd7e69df4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z=-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b16ea246-d4c6-54c5-a144-ce73e8205a6c" :answer/id #uuid "b16ea246-d4c6-54c5-a144-ce73e8205a6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z=-4,4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec0072e3-ac11-5027-8672-d8f98ce2d6a6" :answer/id #uuid "ec0072e3-ac11-5027-8672-d8f98ce2d6a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z=0,-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a26bf7df-34a2-59ce-a047-995bb3f92dd7" :answer/id #uuid "a26bf7df-34a2-59ce-a047-995bb3f92dd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z=0,4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce81a382-d2c4-543c-9aa2-f3d3ef87a4ef" :answer/id #uuid "ce81a382-d2c4-543c-9aa2-f3d3ef87a4ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a8446f82-4129-51d2-b187-039c54ac0008" :question/id #uuid "a8446f82-4129-51d2-b187-039c54ac0008" :question/math-academy-id "q-80057" :question/problem "Solve the equation $3{y}^{2}-3y=2{y}^{2}+y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-3y & =2{y}^{2}+y \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-3y-(2{y}^{2}+y) & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-3y-2{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ (3{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2})+(-3y-y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\ny=0\\,\\text{or}\\,y-4=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\ny=0\\,\\text{or}\\,y=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=0,4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff633b31-e805-5481-a76c-39ad64dfb129"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff633b31-e805-5481-a76c-39ad64dfb129" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff633b31-e805-5481-a76c-39ad64dfb129" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3d9b6c73-c2ec-5d4b-9286-8076ad2a3fb1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b400ce2f-bacb-5dd3-be52-94e8a8b02fd5" "answer-2fedfe26-a02e-52b3-84ea-784270093329" "answer-a990eba7-1df8-5328-abb1-967fc87af018" "answer-3d9b6c73-c2ec-5d4b-9286-8076ad2a3fb1" "answer-f929d974-67f5-5452-a6a4-f6d429ca40a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b400ce2f-bacb-5dd3-be52-94e8a8b02fd5" :answer/id #uuid "b400ce2f-bacb-5dd3-be52-94e8a8b02fd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fedfe26-a02e-52b3-84ea-784270093329" :answer/id #uuid "2fedfe26-a02e-52b3-84ea-784270093329" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-4,0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a990eba7-1df8-5328-abb1-967fc87af018" :answer/id #uuid "a990eba7-1df8-5328-abb1-967fc87af018" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d9b6c73-c2ec-5d4b-9286-8076ad2a3fb1" :answer/id #uuid "3d9b6c73-c2ec-5d4b-9286-8076ad2a3fb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=0,4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f929d974-67f5-5452-a6a4-f6d429ca40a4" :answer/id #uuid "f929d974-67f5-5452-a6a4-f6d429ca40a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186070037 :tutorial/content "To solve a two-term quadratic equation like $x^2 - 7x = 0$ where there is no constant term, we can factor out the variable and apply the zero-product rule.\n\nFactoring out the variable, we have:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tx^2 - 7x &= 0 \\\n\tx(x - 7) &= 0\n}\n$$\n\nWe apply the zero product rule to get \n\n$$\nx=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x-7=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives \n\n$$\nx=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=0,7.$\n\nTo check that these solutions are correct, we can substitute them back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =0 & \\, & \\, & x & =7 \\\\  & ⇓ &  &  &  & ⇓ \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x & =0 &  &  & {x}^{2}-7x & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}-7(0) & =0 &  &  & {7}^{2}-7(7) & =0 \\\\ 0-0 & =0 &  &  & 49-49 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓ &  &  & 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Warning:** It's tempting to solve the equation by dividing by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-7x}{x} & =\\frac{0}{x} \\\\ x-7 & =0 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we do that, we lose the solution $x=0.$ So, when solving equations, *never divide by a variable* because the variable could be zero, and division by zero is not allowed. Instead, try to factor the variable in the equation." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068939 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-20cfcb32-46de-59b7-8990-d20e7a58a53a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-20cfcb32-46de-59b7-8990-d20e7a58a53a" :question/id #uuid "20cfcb32-46de-59b7-8990-d20e7a58a53a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36467" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=0$ gives two lines\n\nII. $c=1$ gives a circle\n\nIII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0$ the level curves are $y=\\pm 2x,$ which consists of two lines.\n\n- If $c=1$ the level curve is $4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements I and III are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-90762acc-754a-5149-93b4-5bc79b9c05e9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-90762acc-754a-5149-93b4-5bc79b9c05e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "90762acc-754a-5149-93b4-5bc79b9c05e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5b93773f-b140-52dc-ab08-af172348d56d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e73ba0dc-dc17-54e6-8c52-71c213062fd1" "answer-5b93773f-b140-52dc-ab08-af172348d56d" "answer-e0f729d2-960c-5f9c-be65-97c3cbad4b0c" "answer-49976768-431c-507e-a6c7-d2d95eebbbf7" "answer-58bb366b-a9d7-55ba-9cad-0fd6b63a25f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e73ba0dc-dc17-54e6-8c52-71c213062fd1" :answer/id #uuid "e73ba0dc-dc17-54e6-8c52-71c213062fd1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b93773f-b140-52dc-ab08-af172348d56d" :answer/id #uuid "5b93773f-b140-52dc-ab08-af172348d56d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0f729d2-960c-5f9c-be65-97c3cbad4b0c" :answer/id #uuid "e0f729d2-960c-5f9c-be65-97c3cbad4b0c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49976768-431c-507e-a6c7-d2d95eebbbf7" :answer/id #uuid "49976768-431c-507e-a6c7-d2d95eebbbf7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58bb366b-a9d7-55ba-9cad-0fd6b63a25f2" :answer/id #uuid "58bb366b-a9d7-55ba-9cad-0fd6b63a25f2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124476 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-248c7282-1f4b-5be4-93d4-cb424b9138b8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-248c7282-1f4b-5be4-93d4-cb424b9138b8" :question/id #uuid "248c7282-1f4b-5be4-93d4-cb424b9138b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-64978" :question/problem "![](images/63/634a863f2154398762af17da40b22d303b10b8846c90fa2021148779e1d1fa97.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=2{x}^{2}$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =c \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{c}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the equations $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{c}{2}}$ do not depend on $y.$ So, for a given $c\\ge 0$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of vertical lines, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/84/844b92278c8b945a68104629b8dcb14d1b41c6875677ffda4da9615363e55221.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-41ce99e3-3910-54c5-acc5-a4b9b347da9d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-41ce99e3-3910-54c5-acc5-a4b9b347da9d" :answer-field/id #uuid "41ce99e3-3910-54c5-acc5-a4b9b347da9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5b8518d8-9401-5fbb-80b4-3637c13a5c3f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f978f806-3849-540f-afee-4d73d3644018" "answer-af41d1db-4df3-5f82-8a52-be9e6a8e128a" "answer-5b8518d8-9401-5fbb-80b4-3637c13a5c3f" "answer-f7438267-12f3-5bc5-8c7f-3213295c7e92" "answer-f930bbbc-48b4-54bf-8000-807c4b6018dc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f978f806-3849-540f-afee-4d73d3644018" :answer/id #uuid "f978f806-3849-540f-afee-4d73d3644018" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/81/81e763cf8b1bf3a6cd2681876642778e97eb4db915373cd07149c42b2e424c65.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af41d1db-4df3-5f82-8a52-be9e6a8e128a" :answer/id #uuid "af41d1db-4df3-5f82-8a52-be9e6a8e128a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/82/824e2fb10927e9ac63fc6e57c15efb94af3100d89a06db8bad63752e232e5a7b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b8518d8-9401-5fbb-80b4-3637c13a5c3f" :answer/id #uuid "5b8518d8-9401-5fbb-80b4-3637c13a5c3f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/84/844b92278c8b945a68104629b8dcb14d1b41c6875677ffda4da9615363e55221.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7438267-12f3-5bc5-8c7f-3213295c7e92" :answer/id #uuid "f7438267-12f3-5bc5-8c7f-3213295c7e92" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/92/9259f6992539edaa9997b167a894fcd8f60ec25978a6bf0c6d7e70b46a5bc056.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f930bbbc-48b4-54bf-8000-807c4b6018dc" :answer/id #uuid "f930bbbc-48b4-54bf-8000-807c4b6018dc" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/fa/fa880cc10c7f9f1532e4fd92093b8d9ff47ab32a220a2c4277ecccabb6788334.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e3fed1a5-4ed0-54b5-88b5-ac6cabd747c7" :activity/id #uuid "e3fed1a5-4ed0-54b5-88b5-ac6cabd747c7" :activity/title "Level Curves" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049882 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-df089dae-31df-5d21-8ad0-f21436e8ebd8" "step-aee806c6-6654-5513-b824-ce14576c7eee" "step-7a044a37-56f7-5838-a701-158e323361de" "step-c004fa3e-a13e-59b0-a5ae-8941190beebd" "step-11b06b48-ff25-5bd7-85cb-a0e8a933ee28"] :activity/first-step "step-df089dae-31df-5d21-8ad0-f21436e8ebd8" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-df089dae-31df-5d21-8ad0-f21436e8ebd8" :step/id #uuid "df089dae-31df-5d21-8ad0-f21436e8ebd8" :step/content "question-20cfcb32-46de-59b7-8990-d20e7a58a53a" :step/next "step-aee806c6-6654-5513-b824-ce14576c7eee" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-aee806c6-6654-5513-b824-ce14576c7eee" :step/id #uuid "aee806c6-6654-5513-b824-ce14576c7eee" :step/content "question-248c7282-1f4b-5be4-93d4-cb424b9138b8" :step/next "step-7a044a37-56f7-5838-a701-158e323361de" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7a044a37-56f7-5838-a701-158e323361de" :step/id #uuid "7a044a37-56f7-5838-a701-158e323361de" :step/content 17592186338935 :step/next "step-c004fa3e-a13e-59b0-a5ae-8941190beebd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c004fa3e-a13e-59b0-a5ae-8941190beebd" :step/id #uuid "c004fa3e-a13e-59b0-a5ae-8941190beebd" :step/content 17592186379910 :step/next "step-11b06b48-ff25-5bd7-85cb-a0e8a933ee28" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-11b06b48-ff25-5bd7-85cb-a0e8a933ee28" :step/id #uuid "11b06b48-ff25-5bd7-85cb-a0e8a933ee28" :step/content 17592186262934 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186137944 :answer-field/choices 17592186137945] [:db/retract 17592186137944 :answer-field/choices 17592186137946] [:db/retract 17592186137944 :answer-field/choices 17592186137947] [:db/retract 17592186137944 :answer-field/choices 17592186137948] [:db/retract 17592186137944 :answer-field/choices 17592186137949] {:db/id 17592186138386 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8dcc5eab-0d17-5264-bead-34e7ca604fff" "question-d4ff2f27-cc03-59a8-bdee-fa30389e7f38"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138298 :question/problem "Factor the expression $x^3 - 4x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x =\nx(x^2 - 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2 - 4$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\nx(x^2 - 4) = x(x+2)(x-2)\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138409 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0a134bf3-f015-57aa-8456-af35dcce2abe" "question-831d09f3-e78e-5131-b5ea-583953555624"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138302 :question/problem "Factor the expression $x^3 - 6x^2 + 9x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 6x^2 + 9x = x(x^2 - 6x + 9)\n$$\n\nThe expression $x^2 - 6x + 9$ is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{2}-6x+9) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}-6x+{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ x(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138410 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-879afca9-fcee-5d1d-bc32-90b5ad8c970f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138303 :question/problem "Factor the expression $x^3 + x^2 - 2x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x & =x({x}^{2}+x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial $x^2 + x - 2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{2}+x-2) & = \\\\ x(({x}^{2}-x)+(2x-2)) & = \\\\ x(x(x-1)+2(x-1)) & = \\\\ x(x+2)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-8dcc5eab-0d17-5264-bead-34e7ca604fff" :question/id #uuid "8dcc5eab-0d17-5264-bead-34e7ca604fff" :question/math-academy-id "q-111921" :question/problem "Factor the expression $9{n}^{4}-{n}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of ${n}^{2}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the ${n}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n9{n}^{4}-{n}^{2}={n}^{2}(9{n}^{2}-1)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $9{n}^{2}-1$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}(9{n}^{2}-1)={n}^{2}((3n)^{2}-{1}^{2})={n}^{2}(3n+1)(3n-1)\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7f4b4fdc-b895-5cda-9128-940ddfc9f191"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7f4b4fdc-b895-5cda-9128-940ddfc9f191" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f4b4fdc-b895-5cda-9128-940ddfc9f191" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-84f9cceb-b824-59c9-bc95-0e9e765459e0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-89058da7-de22-5c1a-89a4-5a2fa2f7d691" "answer-84f9cceb-b824-59c9-bc95-0e9e765459e0" "answer-9044c2ba-d973-5775-b679-1000a32b2258" "answer-3487048b-f83b-5ea7-9fa4-5b3911f15b5c" "answer-92e12dc8-dae9-5034-a85c-9f4ff9be2882"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89058da7-de22-5c1a-89a4-5a2fa2f7d691" :answer/id #uuid "89058da7-de22-5c1a-89a4-5a2fa2f7d691" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n(3n+1)(3n-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84f9cceb-b824-59c9-bc95-0e9e765459e0" :answer/id #uuid "84f9cceb-b824-59c9-bc95-0e9e765459e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{n}^{2}(3n+1)(3n-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9044c2ba-d973-5775-b679-1000a32b2258" :answer/id #uuid "9044c2ba-d973-5775-b679-1000a32b2258" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{n}^{2}(3n+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3487048b-f83b-5ea7-9fa4-5b3911f15b5c" :answer/id #uuid "3487048b-f83b-5ea7-9fa4-5b3911f15b5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{n}^{2}(3n-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92e12dc8-dae9-5034-a85c-9f4ff9be2882" :answer/id #uuid "92e12dc8-dae9-5034-a85c-9f4ff9be2882" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{n}^{4}(3n-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d4ff2f27-cc03-59a8-bdee-fa30389e7f38" :question/id #uuid "d4ff2f27-cc03-59a8-bdee-fa30389e7f38" :question/math-academy-id "q-69586" :question/problem "Factor the expression $9{y}^{5}-{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of ${y}^{3}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the ${y}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n9{y}^{5}-{y}^{3}={y}^{3}(9{y}^{2}-1)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $9{y}^{2}-1$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}(9{y}^{2}-1)={y}^{3}((3y)^{2}-{1}^{2})={y}^{3}(3y+1)(3y-1)\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-207387ef-a367-5acd-bb01-a6190914289d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-207387ef-a367-5acd-bb01-a6190914289d" :answer-field/id #uuid "207387ef-a367-5acd-bb01-a6190914289d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-55a3958e-f134-5356-b4d9-896b8feb884b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6d6b29ae-4b35-5388-a885-7900e7ea04e4" "answer-e15bd179-81cb-57d2-b843-a002a3802601" "answer-56548ac6-5248-51d6-83e6-b18ee6a8a2c8" "answer-55a3958e-f134-5356-b4d9-896b8feb884b" "answer-9b723648-5fcd-553a-94dd-e5b6e26fe68c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d6b29ae-4b35-5388-a885-7900e7ea04e4" :answer/id #uuid "6d6b29ae-4b35-5388-a885-7900e7ea04e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{y}^{3}(3y+1)(y-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e15bd179-81cb-57d2-b843-a002a3802601" :answer/id #uuid "e15bd179-81cb-57d2-b843-a002a3802601" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{y}^{3}(y+1)(y-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56548ac6-5248-51d6-83e6-b18ee6a8a2c8" :answer/id #uuid "56548ac6-5248-51d6-83e6-b18ee6a8a2c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(3y+1)(3y-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55a3958e-f134-5356-b4d9-896b8feb884b" :answer/id #uuid "55a3958e-f134-5356-b4d9-896b8feb884b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{3}(3y+1)(3y-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b723648-5fcd-553a-94dd-e5b6e26fe68c" :answer/id #uuid "9b723648-5fcd-553a-94dd-e5b6e26fe68c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{3}(3y-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0a134bf3-f015-57aa-8456-af35dcce2abe" :question/id #uuid "0a134bf3-f015-57aa-8456-af35dcce2abe" :question/math-academy-id "q-111943" :question/problem "Factor the expression $9{v}^{5}+6{v}^{4}+{v}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of ${v}^{3}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the ${v}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}^{5}+6{v}^{4}+{v}^{3} & ={v}^{3}(9{v}^{2}+6v+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $9{v}^{2}+6v+1$ is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{3}(9{v}^{2}+6v+1) & = \\\\ {v}^{3}((3v)^{2}+6v+{1}^{2}) & = \\\\ {v}^{3}((3v)^{2}+2\\cdot (3v)+{1}^{2}) & = \\\\ {v}^{3}(3v+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4260b274-dc71-5d2d-8a2d-3b8ebca986c4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4260b274-dc71-5d2d-8a2d-3b8ebca986c4" :answer-field/id #uuid "4260b274-dc71-5d2d-8a2d-3b8ebca986c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0b2d79c1-583e-57ab-ba0d-3a6a96d759e0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cbabce0e-ca03-5bbc-b320-a0f7030aa165" "answer-5a5d2964-99a6-545a-8648-910786a6eef4" "answer-87f1c3b3-86a8-589a-a65c-eff5643f2144" "answer-0b2d79c1-583e-57ab-ba0d-3a6a96d759e0" "answer-6412f89c-0a75-5890-ab7c-912727354b37"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbabce0e-ca03-5bbc-b320-a0f7030aa165" :answer/id #uuid "cbabce0e-ca03-5bbc-b320-a0f7030aa165" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{v}^{3}(v+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a5d2964-99a6-545a-8648-910786a6eef4" :answer/id #uuid "5a5d2964-99a6-545a-8648-910786a6eef4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{v}^{2}(v+1)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87f1c3b3-86a8-589a-a65c-eff5643f2144" :answer/id #uuid "87f1c3b3-86a8-589a-a65c-eff5643f2144" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{v}^{2}(3v+1)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b2d79c1-583e-57ab-ba0d-3a6a96d759e0" :answer/id #uuid "0b2d79c1-583e-57ab-ba0d-3a6a96d759e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{v}^{3}(3v+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6412f89c-0a75-5890-ab7c-912727354b37" :answer/id #uuid "6412f89c-0a75-5890-ab7c-912727354b37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{v}^{4}(9v+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-831d09f3-e78e-5131-b5ea-583953555624" :question/id #uuid "831d09f3-e78e-5131-b5ea-583953555624" :question/math-academy-id "q-111945" :question/problem "Factor the expression $4{y}^{4}-12{y}^{3}+9{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of ${y}^{2}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the ${y}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{4}-12{y}^{3}+9{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2}(4{y}^{2}-12y+9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $4{y}^{2}-12y+9$ is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}(4{y}^{2}-12y+9) & = \\\\ {y}^{2}((2y)^{2}-12y+{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ {y}^{2}((2y)^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot (2y)+{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ {y}^{2}(2y-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6990042-c99e-5c76-82b8-8db0cdb93f3b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6990042-c99e-5c76-82b8-8db0cdb93f3b" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6990042-c99e-5c76-82b8-8db0cdb93f3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cf10b3b0-95bb-5d06-8e85-6e049fdba136" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b78343b2-2c20-5528-8530-f2d7808f2d1b" "answer-325b0a34-9436-5b91-8a90-04aec48bede2" "answer-cf10b3b0-95bb-5d06-8e85-6e049fdba136" "answer-b8e3cadc-d863-5dc0-a90a-86254a5b7149" "answer-a538bb6d-1cb1-53f8-a6c2-7b869394a828"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b78343b2-2c20-5528-8530-f2d7808f2d1b" :answer/id #uuid "b78343b2-2c20-5528-8530-f2d7808f2d1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{y}^{4}(2y-9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-325b0a34-9436-5b91-8a90-04aec48bede2" :answer/id #uuid "325b0a34-9436-5b91-8a90-04aec48bede2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{y}^{2}(y-3)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf10b3b0-95bb-5d06-8e85-6e049fdba136" :answer/id #uuid "cf10b3b0-95bb-5d06-8e85-6e049fdba136" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}(2y-3)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8e3cadc-d863-5dc0-a90a-86254a5b7149" :answer/id #uuid "b8e3cadc-d863-5dc0-a90a-86254a5b7149" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{3}(2y-3)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a538bb6d-1cb1-53f8-a6c2-7b869394a828" :answer/id #uuid "a538bb6d-1cb1-53f8-a6c2-7b869394a828" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{4}(4y-9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-879afca9-fcee-5d1d-bc32-90b5ad8c970f" :question/id #uuid "879afca9-fcee-5d1d-bc32-90b5ad8c970f" :question/math-academy-id "q-44321" :question/problem "The trinomial $4{x}^{2}{z}^{6}+8x{z}^{6}-60{z}^{6}$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "Note that all of the terms have a common factor of $4{z}^{6}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $4{z}^{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{z}^{6}+8x{z}^{6}-60{z}^{6}=(4{z}^{6})\\cdot {x}^{2}+(4{z}^{6})\\cdot 2x-(4{z}^{6})\\cdot 15=4{z}^{6}({x}^{2}+2x-15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial ${x}^{2}+2x-15,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{6}({x}^{2}+2x-15) & = \\\\ 4{z}^{6}(({x}^{2}+5x)+(-3x-15)) & = \\\\ 4{z}^{6}(x(x+5)-3(x+5)) & = \\\\ 4{z}^{6}(x-3)(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c9dda1c7-c633-5b09-b7b0-359b74786c15"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c9dda1c7-c633-5b09-b7b0-359b74786c15" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9dda1c7-c633-5b09-b7b0-359b74786c15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-af157abe-2ee3-5b3a-8429-fdcf83a37545" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5f30cf54-4de4-5ad3-b220-c65719f9f268" "answer-2bc95d9d-5eae-5413-94e8-4ae447be363d" "answer-5782bb0d-e372-53c1-b90a-19572aa795a6" "answer-af157abe-2ee3-5b3a-8429-fdcf83a37545" "answer-f38c6d92-f72f-53c8-a611-89911c291beb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f30cf54-4de4-5ad3-b220-c65719f9f268" :answer/id #uuid "5f30cf54-4de4-5ad3-b220-c65719f9f268" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{z}^{6}(x-6)(x+5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bc95d9d-5eae-5413-94e8-4ae447be363d" :answer/id #uuid "2bc95d9d-5eae-5413-94e8-4ae447be363d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{z}^{2}(x-3)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5782bb0d-e372-53c1-b90a-19572aa795a6" :answer/id #uuid "5782bb0d-e372-53c1-b90a-19572aa795a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{z}^{6}(x+3)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af157abe-2ee3-5b3a-8429-fdcf83a37545" :answer/id #uuid "af157abe-2ee3-5b3a-8429-fdcf83a37545" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{z}^{6}(x-3)(x+5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f38c6d92-f72f-53c8-a611-89911c291beb" :answer/id #uuid "f38c6d92-f72f-53c8-a611-89911c291beb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{z}^{6}(z-6)(z+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186139166 :question/problem "Factor the expression $3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x & =x(3{x}^{2}+2x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial $3{x}^{2}+2x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3{x}^{2}+2x-1) & = \\\\ x((3{x}^{2}+3x)+(-x-1)) & = \\\\ x(3x(x+1)-1(x+1)) & = \\\\ x(3x-1)(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186137944 :answer-field/correct "answer-3e67e45d-eca1-5e1d-ab05-8a2c8d157c1d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8e7f324c-5df5-5864-a6e0-aba438969f77" "answer-73678dff-3f54-569b-89c0-a6eceb3259bb" "answer-47926ffc-d7a1-5797-b730-73328c2c7beb" "answer-3e67e45d-eca1-5e1d-ab05-8a2c8d157c1d" "answer-df8b8d89-7aa9-54b3-a94a-df6ecca6f6f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e7f324c-5df5-5864-a6e0-aba438969f77" :answer/id #uuid "8e7f324c-5df5-5864-a6e0-aba438969f77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3x-1)(x+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73678dff-3f54-569b-89c0-a6eceb3259bb" :answer/id #uuid "73678dff-3f54-569b-89c0-a6eceb3259bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3x-1)^{2}(x+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47926ffc-d7a1-5797-b730-73328c2c7beb" :answer/id #uuid "47926ffc-d7a1-5797-b730-73328c2c7beb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(3x+1)(x-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e67e45d-eca1-5e1d-ab05-8a2c8d157c1d" :answer/id #uuid "3e67e45d-eca1-5e1d-ab05-8a2c8d157c1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(3x-1)(x+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df8b8d89-7aa9-54b3-a94a-df6ecca6f6f9" :answer/id #uuid "df8b8d89-7aa9-54b3-a94a-df6ecca6f6f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(3x-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186139645 :tutorial/content "To factor a polynomial, the first step is to find the common factor of all the terms and factor it out. Once we've done that, we can try to apply other factoring tricks that we've learned for quadratic expressions.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $2x^3-2x.$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $2x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $2x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2x^3-2x = 2x(x^2-1)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-1$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n2x(x^2-1) = 2x(x+1)(x-1)\n$$\n\nSo, the expression $2x^3-2x$ factors into $2x(x+1)(x-1).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138638 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186081077 :answer-field/choices 17592186081078] [:db/retract 17592186081077 :answer-field/choices 17592186081079] [:db/retract 17592186081077 :answer-field/choices 17592186081080] [:db/retract 17592186081077 :answer-field/choices 17592186081081] [:db/retract 17592186081077 :answer-field/choices 17592186081082] {:db/id 17592186083284 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a87819e6-d20e-5138-bb0d-a3475ffdb241" "question-72d8f071-152f-5433-92b1-acd921a8a499" "question-8a6dd2d4-a619-5ce5-a81c-14948095cb7a" "question-e424bb65-a367-50ca-a314-7dce8ab99358" "question-4eef4f5e-6db7-5b25-85cf-e831027c6756"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083086 :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as \n\n$$\n0.\\overline{23} = 0.23 + 0.002\\,3 + 0.000\\,023 + \\dots\n$$\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{23} = \\dfrac{23}{100} + \\dfrac{23}{10\\,000} + \\dfrac{23}{1\\,000\\,000}+\\dots\n$$\n\nwhere the first term is $a_1 = \\dfrac{23}{100}$ and the common ratio is $\\dfrac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{23} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{23}{100}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }23\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }23\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{100})}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }23{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083285 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6baf96f3-d002-5d3c-bd1a-08275622d74d" "question-8f09d9a2-0d3b-5003-b95f-e857f12c9e96" "question-8baa6b32-f89a-5746-b5a9-ab0eb10fe7f1" "question-1bafea49-a0df-5bff-bc08-39fb7ee3f6d7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083087 :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1\\overset{―}{4} & =0.144\\,4... \\\\  & =0.1+0.04+0.004+0.000\\,4+… \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+(\\frac{4}{100}+\\frac{4}{1000}+\\frac{4}{10\\,000}+…).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series in the parentheses is a geometric series with the first term $a_1 = \\dfrac{4}{100}$ and the common ratio $r=\\dfrac{1}{10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1\\overset{―}{4} & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4\\cdot 10}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{10}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a87819e6-d20e-5138-bb0d-a3475ffdb241" :question/id #uuid "a87819e6-d20e-5138-bb0d-a3475ffdb241" :question/math-academy-id "q-351711" :question/problem "Express $6.\\overset{―}{6}$ as a geometric series.\n\n$6.\\overset{―}{6}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$)^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6.\\overset{―}{6} & =6.666… \\\\  & =6+0.6+0.06+0.006+… \\\\  & ={{6+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{6}{100}+\\frac{6}{1\\,000}+⋯.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term is ${a}_{1}=6$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6.\\overset{―}{6} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }60(\\frac{1}{10}){(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }60{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6374aaa6-2efd-5ae0-8e84-968f57459ea3" "answer-field-b23ddbb5-4c49-53e8-aff0-51e2735da1f5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6374aaa6-2efd-5ae0-8e84-968f57459ea3" :answer-field/id #uuid "6374aaa6-2efd-5ae0-8e84-968f57459ea3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-56d7b83d-af0c-5f10-9c25-945baac62363" :answer-field/choices ["answer-56d7b83d-af0c-5f10-9c25-945baac62363"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56d7b83d-af0c-5f10-9c25-945baac62363" :answer/id #uuid "56d7b83d-af0c-5f10-9c25-945baac62363" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60" :db/